WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:42.600
Welcome and Opening Comments
Malcolm Grant , Chairman: I am Malcolm Grant,

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:49.409
the chairman of NHS England, and I want initially
to thank everybody for coming tonight and

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.860
to welcome you to this, our Annual General
Meeting.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.899
I think it is quite remarkable that the NHS
is loved so warmly across the country and

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:05.899
that so many people are willing and interested
to come and engage with this board. Understand

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:14.509
that the AGM is still an innovation for the
NHS. Last year, we held the first ever Annual

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:20.290
General Meeting for the NHS. I know that many
of you were here on that occasion, and I am

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:26.480
grateful to you for staying with us and coming
back tonight. By the way, the NHS does not

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:33.099
belong to the board. The NHS belongs to the
people of England. By having an open - there

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:41.339
was a yes. Thank you very much. Yes, yes.
Thank you. I am fully expecting the other

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.010
side of those cards at various points during
the evening, but I am so pleased to start

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:47.430
off with the right one.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:54.430
Let me be very serious about this. The exercise
in which we have been involved all day, and

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:02.200
about which we will hear some more in a moment,
has been educational, to use a neutral term,

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:09.319
but also quite moving for members of the board.
To listen to patients and the public and carers

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:16.319
and those who are involved in the day-to-day
work of the NHS; to understand what our dedicated

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:23.370
workforce across England do well; and to understand
where there are points of frustration and

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.639
difficulty with patients and carers.

00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:30.590
Programme of events
Let me start by outlining the format for this

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:37.590
evening's events. The way in which we would
like to do things this evening is to start

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:17.079
with something which I know everybody will
gain a great deal from, which is presentations

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:24.079
from today's NHS Citizens' Assembly. Those
will be led by Olivia Butterworth, who I will

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:34.060
introduce in a moment. Secondly, there will
then be the formal business of the AGM. It

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:40.370
is not the normal blah, blah, blah. There
are ways in which corporates hold AGMs in

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:47.370
which a board sit on public display and answer
questions about the chief executive's remuneration.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:56.430
That is not our style. We do not have shareholders.
We have you: the public of England. We operate

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:08.380
within a political environment, and we are
spending public money. That is why I am most

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:15.380
determined that the board of NHS England should,
at its AGM, account to those who are taking

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.409
an interest in our affairs.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:24.300
At 18.00 I will talk again. I will be followed
by Simon Stevens, our Chief Executive, and

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:31.120
then our Chief Financial Officer, Paul Baumann,
will outline the accounts which we are presenting

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.880
to you tonight. After that, I will have an
open question and answer session, and I will

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.490
outline some proposed rules of engagement
before we go into that part of the proceedings.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:48.770
Without more ado, I am delighted to introduce
Olivia Butterworth and to ask her to facilitate

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.380
the next phase of this evening's event.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:50.840
NHS Citizen
Olivia Butterworth: Okay, thank you very much.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.569
My involvement in NHS England is to facilitate
conversations between people in power and

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:51.840
people who use the NHS. Over the last year,
we have been designing, in public, something

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:52.090
we have called NHS Citizen. We do not own
it or control it; it is out with people. It

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.080
is open for anyone to participate. If you
want to have a look at it, it is nhscitizen.org.uk.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:16.250
Everything we have done today has been worldwide
webcast. Today has been the culmination of

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:36.729
testing a process about how we bring people's
voices in, and doing it in a way in which

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:16.979
try to change the conversation. We are trying
to change the conversation to one of dialogue

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:22.370
where we bring people with power - people
on the board or in decision-making roles - together

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.990
with citizens to have a shared conversation
about the issues that matter. We have had

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:30.523
five conversations today that every board
member of NHS England has participated in,

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.669
and we have had some 250 people from every
bit of the country involved in these conversations.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:31.340
These four citizens are going to try and reflect
some of those conversations that those 250

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:32.460
people have had today.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:39.300
That is quite nerve-racking, so before they
do that I am going to look to the executive

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.840
directors who were part of those conversations
to give us their reflections on what they

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:50.729
have heard today. Then we are going to try
and turn it into a conversation about what

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:57.729
is next and what it actually means. Our citizens
are going to try and reflect whether or not

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:02.379
that feels like a fair reflection that they
can connect with in terms of whether they

00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:02.680
have been heard or not.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:09.360
I am going to look first to Tim, the National
Director for Patients and Information, to

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.159
give us his reflections on a conversation
that he was part of around self-care and personalisation.

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:20.159
Tim Kelsey: At the root of this and, I suspect,
of many other sessions was a fundamental issue

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:27.370
that we have all got, which is about making
sure that people and patients and professionals

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:37.009
have a real voice in the design and delivery
of local services. This was about giving people

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:42.409
real control, when they want it, of their
own care; making sure that they were able

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:49.409
to express when things went well or badly;
and to ensure that we had real transparency

00:08:50.089 --> 00:08:54.819
in the way in which the local health service
operated. Not just proper accountability but

00:08:54.819 --> 00:09:01.819
meaningful, real, personal responses to need
in local communities. Broadly, that was the

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:07.390
overarching theme, I think. I will be interested
to see if colleagues here thought that was

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:08.050
the case.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:12.680
There were some specific things that I think
NHS England will absolutely need to really

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:19.680
focus on, and I hope we will do so. The first
was recognising that this is a truly communal

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:26.800
endeavour. Our NHS is everybody's NHS, and
in that context there are some communities

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:32.620
that I do not think we are very good at offering
the support they need. Many of the voices

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:39.620
in the group I was in would have said the
same thing. In particular, carers were the

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:46.870
focal point for many of the conversations.
What we heard there was that NHS England must

00:09:47.579 --> 00:09:54.569
give professionals and others the permission
to train carers, support them and recognise

00:09:54.569 --> 00:09:59.610
their value in the process of delivering care.
That is not just about recognising that they

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.650
are real assets for patients that they are
looking after, but also because we need to

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:09.610
care for the carers. That was a really strong
message that came through that session - unleashing

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:16.069
the power of people, and particularly focusing
our efforts on those people who are caring,

00:10:16.069 --> 00:10:20.290
seems to be a really important thing that
we can do something about, and I think we

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:23.069
can commit to making that much more real.

00:10:23.069 --> 00:10:29.449
I am rushing slightly, but the excellent graphic
on the wall there gives a more detailed summary

00:10:29.449 --> 00:10:30.290
of what was said.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:36.870
The second area that I took away was why does
the NHS not operate to the same standard that

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:42.620
we are used to in the rest of our lives, giving
us the advantages that digital technology

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:46.689
offer. We have mobile phones and are able
to do all sorts of things for ourselves. We

00:10:46.689 --> 00:10:51.829
are able to take control, but the NHS does
not offer us the same opportunity, when we

00:10:51.829 --> 00:10:58.280
want it, at the same scale. There was a big
ask around making apps more useful to patients,

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:04.319
for example, or the NHS actually deciding
which apps are safe and good, and making some

00:11:04.319 --> 00:11:10.030
level of recommendation about that to the
public, to help people find digital services

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:14.240
when they want to. So there was a piece there
about technology, and I think we can take

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.069
that away and do something about that.

00:11:18.069 --> 00:11:25.069
Finally, there was a consensus around the
vital importance of this kind of meeting and,

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:32.990
for those who were earlier, the importance
of NHS Citizen as a means of creating a kind

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:39.419
of conversation which needs to be ongoing
and real for all of us and, In that context,

00:11:39.419 --> 00:11:44.000
a commitment to really work hard on learning
the lessons of today and what NHS Citizen

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:50.350
has told us, and to come back with ideas shared
across the community about how we can truly

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:56.010
build voice into the way NHS England operates,
and how we can collectively learn and work

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.709
together better.

00:11:57.709 --> 00:12:00.329
Thank you.

00:12:00.329 --> 00:12:06.890
Olivia Butterworth: Some real commitments
to actually taking action. I think we might

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:10.079
come back to you about what some of those
tangible actions might be.

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:16.770
Ian, I am going to look to you next. Ian Dodge
is a new national director to NHS England

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:20.559
whose job title I cannot remember. Perhaps
you can remind us.

00:12:20.559 --> 00:12:25.199
Ian Dodge: Thank you, Olivia. I am National
Director of Commissioning Strategy in NHS

00:12:25.199 --> 00:12:29.679
England, and I have just been let out of a
dungeon in the Department of Health where

00:12:29.679 --> 00:12:34.230
I have been locked up for the last 20 years.
I am delighted to be here today, and I was

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:41.230
in a conversation about access to services.
I heard a lot of frustration from citizens

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:49.549
around the problems that some of our most
marginalised people in society have in accessing

00:12:49.549 --> 00:12:56.549
care, whether it is people who are deaf, people
from black and minority ethnic groups, Gypsies

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:04.810
- a whole raft of people who we know tend
to find it harder to access normal services.

00:13:05.059 --> 00:13:11.089
And then, when they do access those services,
they tend to use them less well and receive

00:13:11.089 --> 00:13:18.089
less of them than more pushy, middle-class
white people. I think that message came across

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:26.750
loud and clear. There was also something that
gave me grounds for hope - a lot of energy

00:13:28.419 --> 00:13:34.669
and desire, particularly from people who were
working in voluntary organisations, to say

00:13:34.669 --> 00:13:40.079
that actually some of the solutions are with
them.

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:46.120
Some of this is about mind-set change. How
do we move away from the sense that the only

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:53.120
way in which value can be added to people's
health and healthcare is by professionals

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:59.660
providing that care? How can we get more involved?
How can we have a different conversation,

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:05.040
help with the access issues, and help with
the care issues? I think there is an understanding

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:12.040
that it is not that GP practices and hospitals
are wilfully failing to engage their communities

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:17.780
effectively. It is that they are really busy
and under a lot of pressure. There was a really

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:23.410
big question about how we can get to a place
where there is a better dialogue and a better

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:29.380
conversation between people that are buying
and people that are providing care, and the

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:34.850
citizens and the communities that they serve?
We do not know what the right answers to that

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:38.260
are, but we know that there is a lot more
that needs to be done.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:45.260
I think it is incumbent on us as the board
members of NHS England to think about what

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:50.470
this means in terms of our own behaviour.
In the two months I have been in NHS England,

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:56.020
very candidly, there have probably been only
two occasions, of which today is one, where

00:14:56.020 --> 00:15:01.990
I have actually had long conversations with
citizens, rather than just conversations with

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:08.990
fellow technocrats in the NHS system. There
is actually something about my own behaviour

00:15:09.179 --> 00:15:16.000
and that of people that work for me. There
is something about our strategy and what we

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:23.000
say, so we are planning to produce a forward
view for the NHS over the next five years

00:15:23.339 --> 00:15:28.679
in the autumn. And there is something about
how we then work with clinical commissioning

00:15:28.679 --> 00:15:34.270
groups and boards of NHS organisations to
encourage them to engage more effectively

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:35.339
with their communities.

00:15:35.339 --> 00:15:36.860
Thank you.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:43.860
Olivia Butterworth: There is a real theme
coming through about using and working with

00:15:44.789 --> 00:15:48.750
and alongside people, and the assets in our
communities, and recognising that if we do

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:54.870
things differently we can connect things up
better for people. You talked a lot about

00:15:54.870 --> 00:16:00.510
harder-to-reach groups, and I wonder, Barbara
Hakin, if I can look to you to feed back on

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:06.130
quite a specific conversation around transgender
people and where that got to?

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:11.829
Barbara Hakin: Thanks, Olivia. Hello, everybody.
I was in a group today that was talking about

00:16:11.829 --> 00:16:18.049
the services that we have for patients with
transgender issues - gender dysphoria. Of

00:16:18.049 --> 00:16:23.959
course, a lot of the individuals in that group
had gender dysphoria themselves, or they were

00:16:23.959 --> 00:16:28.410
carers or parents or relatives of such individuals.

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:34.000
The first thing that struck me, to be honest,
was that we really could do much better. We

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.640
could provide much better services for those
individuals. But what really overwhelmed me

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:45.640
in the room is that, despite that, everybody
there was nice, kind and pleasant to me. There

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:52.949
could have been recriminations that the services
were not good enough, but there were all sorts

00:16:52.949 --> 00:16:59.949
of suggestions about what we could do - lots
of ideas - but all said in a way that was

00:17:00.419 --> 00:17:05.959
so nice and respectful. I want to thank you
all for that, but what I want to say to that

00:17:05.959 --> 00:17:11.539
group of people and to everybody is, would
that I had a magic wand that would make every

00:17:11.539 --> 00:17:17.539
contact in the NHS that we have with our patients
as pleasant and courteous as the contacts

00:17:17.539 --> 00:17:19.089
that we had in that room today.

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:24.209
That was the other big message that was coming
out of that group: that the first problem

00:17:24.209 --> 00:17:30.200
that they had was that people did not treat
individuals in the way that we would all want

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:34.270
to be treated ourselves. We really, absolutely
have to do something about that, and I think

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:37.620
that has been a message out of today. It is
not just about listening, which is vital and

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:43.220
important. It is about being nice: courtesy,
dignity and respect.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:48.010
A lot of the issues that came out were about
general practice and that first, and absolutely

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:53.720
vital, contact that patients have with their
general practitioner. I was a GP myself for

00:17:53.720 --> 00:18:00.720
20 years before I moved over to the dark side
of management, as some would say. Believe

00:18:01.169 --> 00:18:07.740
me, they are both absolutely invaluable things
to do to support patients to get a better

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:12.080
service. While we all know that there are
lots of really brilliant GPs who are giving

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:16.080
patients a really great service, there were
also stories that none of us would be happy

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:23.080
about. Even where the GPs were really determined
to do the best they could for these patients,

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:29.000
there was evidence that information, knowledge
and easy access to next steps was so difficult

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:29.490
for them.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:36.080
Out of that, I need to make a commitment to
do a couple of things. Firstly, to work with

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:40.889
those who are involved in GP education - the
Royal Colleges and various others - to say,

00:18:40.889 --> 00:18:45.379
for this particular group of individuals,
how can we make sure that GPs have readily

00:18:45.379 --> 00:18:50.539
accessible information? And how can we find
some middle ground? How can we find some people

00:18:50.539 --> 00:18:53.580
who are more expert and can support people
locally, without them always having to travel

00:18:53.580 --> 00:19:00.059
a very long distance? They may have to do
that for very specialist services, but how

00:19:00.059 --> 00:19:05.970
can we find something that bridges that gap?
So that would be the first part: the early

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:06.659
GP services.

00:19:06.659 --> 00:19:12.539
We then went on to talk about the more specialised
services - the very specialised end of these

00:19:12.539 --> 00:19:19.539
services - and again, I took a lot from that
which will help me in the things I do across

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:26.860
a full range of specialised services for patients
or individuals who have less common issues

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:34.230
or disorders, to which we have to pay particular
attention because it is so much more difficult

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:40.029
for them to have a voice. I heard about a
really good clinical reference group which

00:19:40.029 --> 00:19:47.029
is looking at what the nature of the best
care could be, and a good stakeholder group

00:19:47.259 --> 00:19:52.590
of patients, carers and individuals who are
working with them. There was some concern

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:57.950
about whether we would be able to maintain
that group and particularly its stakeholder

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.840
support, so something else I will take away
is to go and look at that and find out how

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:08.840
we can make sure that that action and that
vital connection keeps going.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:15.519
The final thing to take away, really, is just
the inequalities. Services such as this, if

00:20:15.519 --> 00:20:22.519
they are not really the best they can be,
can exacerbate inequalities. We need not only

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:30.320
to look at the general services for individuals
who have one issue, but actually to look at

00:20:31.679 --> 00:20:38.360
them as a whole person and look at the aspects
of their lives, particularly in this group.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:43.570
Individuals with gender dysphoria who are
from black and minority ethnic groups, or

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:50.570
children and adolescents, particularly as
they transfer to being adults, were worrying

00:20:50.649 --> 00:20:57.490
deeply in terms of letting individuals down.
Again, I will take that away, because every

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:02.639
individual deserves the rights that are in
the NHS constitution, and we need to work

00:21:02.639 --> 00:21:07.799
really hard to make sure that everybody gets
those rights and that the inequalities that

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:14.799
we all know about are addressed.

00:21:18.799 --> 00:21:19.100
Thank you.

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:22.669
Olivia Butterworth: Thanks very much, Barbara.
Again, another theme around equality, but

00:21:22.669 --> 00:21:27.480
particularly around stigma and how we challenge
stigma, and how we improve that communication

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.200
flow and really connect with people to really
understand them.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:36.700
Sir Bruce Keogh, I am going to come to you
next. You had some conversations this afternoon

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:41.340
around mental health services. I wonder if
you would like to give us your reflections.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:45.840
Bruce Keogh: Thanks very much. It is 18.00
on a Thursday evening, so thank you all very

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:51.139
much for coming and for staying. I think it
has been a really important event.

00:21:51.139 --> 00:21:57.629
The session that I went to was focused around
mental health, and the nature of the discussion

00:21:57.629 --> 00:22:04.629
was very constructive, but sadly it was based
on enormous frustration with the nature and

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:10.789
disposition of services that were provided
for patients. Having said that, I left with

00:22:10.789 --> 00:22:17.379
a fairly optimistic point of view, feeling
that in many ways the time has come when we

00:22:17.379 --> 00:22:22.169
can solve many of the problems that were in
that room. I think there are a constellation

00:22:22.169 --> 00:22:28.549
of things that have come together to help
us do that. In many ways, mental health has

00:22:28.549 --> 00:22:34.620
been a hostage to the history of the way services
have developed. In my background as a heart

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:40.279
surgeon over many years, we would put patients
through some pretty gruesome things - big

00:22:40.279 --> 00:22:45.580
operations, a long time in the intensive care
unit. Not surprisingly, a number of them would

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:52.559
need some kind of support for that, and yet
it was never forthcoming. Why was it not forthcoming?

00:22:52.559 --> 00:22:56.990
Because mental health services and support
were provided in some other building in some

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:03.990
other place it was a hassle to get to. Equally,
my colleagues who were trying to provide psychiatric

00:23:04.179 --> 00:23:09.480
and other services were increasingly beginning
to recognise that they needed help from those

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:14.610
that provided physical services, and yet they
were not getting that support.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:19.600
Things are starting to change. The science
has changed. There is a much greater recognition

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:24.759
now that there is a very close association
between mental health issues and physical

00:23:24.759 --> 00:23:31.759
health issues. Furthermore, there is a very,
very clear and strong economic case that,

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:38.820
as a country and a health service, we need
to focus on mental health. Add to that the

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:45.820
fact that there are several senior people
in NHS England, myself included, who have

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:51.690
very close familial associations with serious
mental health problems. They would be quite

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:56.429
happy for me to say that. We recognise the
issues, and we are, in fact, determined to

00:23:56.429 --> 00:24:01.059
deal with them. I think we have come to a
point where the science, the economics and

00:24:01.059 --> 00:24:08.059
the aspirations of patients are starting to
match. That is a pretty strong driver for

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:12.149
change, I think.

00:24:12.149 --> 00:24:17.259
Within the forum that we were in this afternoon,
a number of issues came up. Of course, I will

00:24:17.259 --> 00:24:22.779
not be able to cover them all, but the first
was the issue of access to mental health services.

00:24:22.779 --> 00:24:29.129
I have alluded to that already, but in particular
there is a feeling, as one person put it,

00:24:29.129 --> 00:24:34.749
that when you are in a very bad mental health
state and you ring up and they say, it is

00:24:34.749 --> 00:24:40.080
going to be six months before you are seen,
it is a bit like ringing 999 and them saying

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:46.919
they will send a policeman round in a couple
of weeks. This is a serious problem, and we

00:24:46.919 --> 00:24:52.710
need to go away and think about how we make
it much easier and much more timely for people

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:56.049
to get help when they need it.

00:24:56.049 --> 00:25:02.919
The second thing is the so-called parity of
esteem - my apologies to Roger Goss. That

00:25:02.919 --> 00:25:08.799
effectively means that we need to put as much
time and attention into developing services

00:25:08.799 --> 00:25:15.799
for mental health as we do into physical health.
That takes us into areas of education, particularly

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:22.619
for many of the professionals in our services
- doctors, nurses and others - some of whom

00:25:22.619 --> 00:25:27.759
have not been that good at recognising the
association between mental health problems

00:25:27.759 --> 00:25:34.159
and standard medical problems. That then leads
us into how we design our services in a way

00:25:34.159 --> 00:25:40.350
that makes it easier for people to get both
physical and mental health help when they

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:41.440
need it.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:48.279
The next thing which was really quite striking
was that services are very disjointed. We

00:25:48.279 --> 00:25:54.179
heard a number of stories from people who
felt that their mental health condition had

00:25:54.179 --> 00:26:01.179
been made much worse by the utter frustration,
anger and anxiety that they felt being bounced

00:26:01.669 --> 00:26:07.019
from pillar to post like a pinball in a pinball
machine. They felt that it actually made matters

00:26:07.019 --> 00:26:14.019
worse. We have a duty in terms of our humanitarian
approach, our clinical approach and our organisational

00:26:18.029 --> 00:26:24.850
approach to ensure that the way we design
services and help others design services does

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:28.119
not make people's conditions worse.

00:26:28.119 --> 00:26:35.119
Finally, there were comments about peer support.
It seemed clear to me, leaving the room this

00:26:36.529 --> 00:26:42.980
afternoon, that there is a lot we can do to
help bring people together. As someone put

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:49.039
it, we use talking therapies in a clinical
way, but there is nothing better than talking

00:26:49.039 --> 00:26:55.320
to people with similar problems to help you
resolve your own. There is a lot of evidence

00:26:55.320 --> 00:27:01.039
in the physical world that peer support helps
when people with diabetes talk about it, or

00:27:01.039 --> 00:27:05.139
people with rheumatoid arthritis get together.
Similarly, there are big opportunities we

00:27:05.139 --> 00:27:09.720
can exploit in mental health.

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:16.720
Probably the final area that we touched on
was the growing mental health problems of

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:23.499
the younger population, who are subject to
a lot of external stresses which older members

00:27:23.499 --> 00:27:30.499
of the audience might not have experienced,
through much greater connectivity that is

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:37.590
afforded through the social media and others.
We in NHS England are in the process of completely

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:43.269
looking at how we provide those services in
a more effective way.

00:27:43.269 --> 00:27:50.269
I leave you, I think, with an optimistic thought:
a number of things have come together that

00:27:50.639 --> 00:27:57.639
mean that we will be able to report back to
you in a year's time that we have made at

00:27:58.409 --> 00:28:05.409
least some, if not considerable, progress.
Thank you.

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.970
Olivia Butterworth: Thanks very much, Bruce.
I am going to move very swiftly on to the

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:15.659
last workshop feedback. Jane Cummings is our
Chief Nurse, and she was talking with a group

00:28:15.659 --> 00:28:21.279
of children and young people. Picking up on
Bruce's last point about young people's access

00:28:21.279 --> 00:28:26.730
to mental health services, I think that was
quite a hot topic in your group as well.

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:29.340
Jane Cummings: Thank you, Olivia. Good evening,
everybody.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:34.590
It was an incredibly powerful and brilliant
session, actually, that I was privileged to

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:41.590
be at this afternoon. I think a lot of what
we have heard already was replicated in the

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:47.220
group that we were in with children and young
people. I suppose that is inevitable, because

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:51.269
looking at all of the other issues that my
colleagues have spoken about is also something

00:28:51.269 --> 00:28:57.090
that is very meaningful for children and young
people. As a group - as a room - it was very

00:28:57.090 --> 00:29:03.950
powerful and very positive. It was very vibrant
and energetic. Everybody that was in that

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:10.950
room felt and contributed significantly with
ideas about what should and could happen to

00:29:11.919 --> 00:29:16.999
make things better, but also shared their
personal experiences and the experiences of

00:29:16.999 --> 00:29:23.999
others, which highlighted with significant
clarity - and quite a lot of difficulty, actually,

00:29:25.649 --> 00:29:32.649
to hear - what was going well and what was
not going well. Thank you very much to those

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:36.779
young people who are people to share those
stories. Also, it was something that made

00:29:36.779 --> 00:29:43.169
it very meaningful in terms of what we are
trying to do and what we need to do.

00:29:43.169 --> 00:29:48.529
I will try and summarise some of the themes
that I heard discussed, and then talk a little

00:29:48.529 --> 00:29:53.980
bit about some of the things I think we can
do. We may want to come back to that a bit

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:54.389
later.

00:29:54.389 --> 00:29:59.809
One of the first areas that people talked
about was transition - the transition from

00:29:59.809 --> 00:30:06.059
being cared for as a child or a young person
into being an adult. There was a significant

00:30:06.059 --> 00:30:12.710
amount of frustration about how difficult
that was for them. Not only are you often

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:18.980
facing a transition at a time when things
are quite difficult, doing exams at school,

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:23.049
preparing for university and being a teenager,
with all the normal things that happen with

00:30:23.049 --> 00:30:27.799
teenagers. I have got two teenage nieces at
the moment, so I am living and breathing that

00:30:27.799 --> 00:30:33.299
with them on a day-to-day basis. There are
issues around how the transition can happen

00:30:33.299 --> 00:30:37.929
at different ages. In some cases, it will
be in the run-up to being 18. In others, it

00:30:37.929 --> 00:30:42.179
might be when they are slightly older, in
their early twenties.

00:30:42.179 --> 00:30:48.720
The big issue - a really significant issue
- is how the criteria for care change. Young

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:54.690
children or young people would be entitled
to, and get, support in an area when they

00:30:54.690 --> 00:31:00.970
were seen as a child or a young person, but
as soon as they transitioned into an adult,

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:06.070
that disappeared and the criteria changed,
and therefore they could not access the same

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:11.960
level of service. We heard about somebody
who had had a coordinator - a senior nurse

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:16.989
who was able to coordinate all of the care
that he needed and link in with all of the

00:31:16.989 --> 00:31:22.639
specialties that he might need to come into
contact with. He could make one phone call

00:31:22.639 --> 00:31:29.389
and he was supported to get that care. Once
he became an adult, or was seen as a young

00:31:29.389 --> 00:31:34.279
adult, that disappeared. It was then down
to him to make multiple phone calls and be

00:31:34.279 --> 00:31:38.690
passed, as others have said, from pillar to
post before finding the right person to support

00:31:38.690 --> 00:31:43.759
the ongoing care. Quite frankly, none of us
would want that to happen for ourselves or

00:31:43.759 --> 00:31:47.700
for our loved ones, and so we need to do something
about making that better.

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:54.259
There was a lot about voice and about being
heard and being listened to. One of the most

00:31:54.259 --> 00:31:59.989
powerful things I heard somebody say was that
adults do not know everything - they might

00:31:59.989 --> 00:32:06.690
think they do, but they do not - and maturity
is not based on age. You can have a young

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:12.340
person who is incredibly mature and able to
discuss what they need and what they want,

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:19.340
and we do not listen enough. Quality of care
can be variable across the system.

00:32:19.389 --> 00:32:24.379
We talked a lot about quality of services,
access to services, being able to get the

00:32:24.379 --> 00:32:29.720
care needed at the time it was needed and
not having to wait. There were things like

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:34.789
physically being able to access services in
a wheelchair etc. - all of the things that

00:32:34.789 --> 00:32:39.850
should be there should be there for everybody
when they need it. There was a big theme about

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:46.850
joining things up, and a lot of discussion
about the link between health and education.

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:53.139
Why is it that education are not aware of
some of the health needs of children and young

00:32:53.139 --> 00:32:57.879
people? Why is it that education will not
listen, will not get engaged and will not

00:32:57.879 --> 00:33:02.429
support? Why is that, in some cases, the health
service will not discuss issues and will not

00:33:02.429 --> 00:33:04.720
help and support education to do better?

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:09.409
There were also issues about joining up between
health services and making sure that there

00:33:09.409 --> 00:33:15.399
is that coordination and, importantly, between
health and social care. Something that really

00:33:15.399 --> 00:33:21.259
talks about looking at the person as an individual
and wrapping services around them, rather

00:33:21.259 --> 00:33:24.690
than expecting individuals to have to try
and find their way through.

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:31.690
There was a big issue about value and respect.
We heard stories where people were talking

00:33:31.929 --> 00:33:38.929
about not being listened to or being talked
over - health professionals talking to parents

00:33:39.139 --> 00:33:46.139
or carers when the child or young person was
perfectly capable and willing, and wanted

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:51.230
to be able to tell their story, to talk about
how they felt and to describe what they needed.

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:57.080
This is a sense that, because they were young,
it did not matter and that the healthcare

00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:59.789
professionals should and could talk to parents.

00:33:59.789 --> 00:34:03.369
A really big issue for me, and one of the
things that I committed myself to doing in

00:34:03.369 --> 00:34:08.460
the room, was using my influence across the
nursing and midwifery profession, and working

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:12.369
with Bruce - he does not know this yet, but
I will tell him now - around the medical profession

00:34:12.369 --> 00:34:17.530
to reinforce the importance of talking to
people as individuals and actually valuing,

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:22.560
and listening to, what they have to say, whatever
their age.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:28.460
We talked a lot about diversity and individuality.
Barbara talked about the transgender issue.

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:34.750
We heard a very, very powerful story about
a young person and the issues that you face

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:40.690
if you are lesbian, gay or transgender. Those
issues - having gender dysphoria and actually

00:34:40.690 --> 00:34:46.280
being able to get access to services and talk
to people who understand, who know what you

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:52.010
are talking about, who listen to you and who
will actually refer you to the right services.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:56.240
Lastly, it was about not forgetting the outcomes.
It is all very well talking about input measures

00:34:56.240 --> 00:35:00.619
and about making sure that we do that, but
make sure that actually what we are doing,

00:35:00.619 --> 00:35:05.170
and the care and support we put in place,
is something that is meaningful to the individual

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:09.800
child or young person and that it delivers
the best outcomes for them, the best experience,

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:13.380
the best care and the best safe care.

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:19.470
In terms of what we talked about, I have already
mentioned the professional agenda - making

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:26.100
sure that healthcare professionals actually
treat people as individuals, listen to what

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:31.540
children and young people say and talk to
them, not about them. Also, there is engaging

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:38.040
and living what we say are our values. Effectively,
if we say we want to do co-production, and

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:43.940
if we say we want to listen, then truly do
that. Hear what people say. Ask them to help

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:50.940
us design what we want to do and design our
services and co-produce. Actually, as somebody

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:58.110
said to us, they are vesting a lot of interest
and trust in us, so show us and demonstrate

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.670
that we actually mean what we say and that
we will do something.

00:36:01.670 --> 00:36:04.370
Thank you.

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:11.370
Simon Burrell: Jane, thank you very much.
Thank you all very much. I am one of the team

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:25.790
of four organisations that is helping NHS
England to design NHS Citizen. I am really

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:29.250
not going to speak for very long. I have been
one of the two people facilitating the whole

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:35.140
day. We have had 250 citizens sitting down
and taking their own time, and we have asked

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:41.580
these four to come up - very, very last-minute
- as citizens to give their personal view

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:46.300
of what they heard. Specifically, I am going
to ask them two things. One, does what you

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:52.520
have just head from the directors reflect
what you heard in the room for the whole day?

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:57.180
And secondly, is what they say they are going
to do enough?

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:01.050
Emma has to go at quarter past, so I will
apologise on your behalf for having to leave

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:06.630
the stage. We are very sorry to have kept
you, but we thank you so much. I am going

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:08.410
to hand over to you.

00:37:08.410 --> 00:37:15.410
Emma Rowbuck[?]: Thank you for your kind words,
Barbara. Yes, it is reflective of what was

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:25.780
said in the room. It was nicely distilled,
and it spoke to a lot of the issues. I did

00:37:29.990 --> 00:37:36.380
speak earlier on, and I said that the issues
are far, far greater than a day's discussion.

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:41.140
I think it is a beginning - a good beginning
- and it is great that the board have actually

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:47.210
been there and listened. It would be great
if the board did some meaningful, fantastic

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:54.210
interventions for all four areas, and other
areas, to be honest.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:38:01.950
Some very clear and prescient issues were
raised around waiting, and the inequalities

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:09.320
of waiting for trans people - waiting for
services, and waiting for appropriate services,

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:15.020
rather than, as a friend of mine calls it,
a sausage factory where you plug somebody

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:22.020
in at one end and you get a different gender
out the other end. A holistic process that

00:38:23.270 --> 00:38:30.270
works for people. There are some key areas
around the seven gender identity clinics around

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:34.940
the country at the moment where these is a
disparity, and hopefully the new protocol

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:41.310
will give some resolution to that. This means
nothing to you, I suspect.

00:38:41.310 --> 00:38:48.310
What I would like to see, hopefully, is that
the board go away and listen and maybe look

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:54.670
at some of the current issues that we discussed
around particular waiting lists in certain

00:38:54.670 --> 00:39:01.230
areas, and lubricate the wheels, I think is
the right way of doing it, in a diplomatic,

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:08.230
strategic, pressurised way. For me, this is
a fantastic start, and I am genuinely not

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:17.800
trying to blow smoke. It is a fantastic start
when these people have actually taken the

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:23.280
time to come. It would have been very, very
easy for them as board members to sit in the

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:30.230
boardroom and the various places where the
DOH and NHS England reside across the country.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:37.230
They have taken the time to listen to us,
and I do appreciate that, but I hope it elicits

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:45.910
genuine actions for a very, very marginalised
group of people.

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:52.950
Simon Burrell: Emma, in a perfect illustration
that the NHS does not work around people's

00:39:55.380 --> 00:40:01.940
lives, Emma has to go to catch a train. You
will now leave the stage. Fiona, I am going

00:40:01.940 --> 00:40:07.350
to hand over to you. Did what you heard reflect
what you heard in the room, and is the action

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:08.850
enough?

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:15.850
Olivia Butterworth: Just to intervene, because
I appreciate that people did not necessarily

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:21.010
know who Emma was, she works at Gay Advice
Darlington, and she has been here as a citizen

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:26.480
today, participating in those conversations.
Fiona, perhaps you could just say who you

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:29.550
are very briefly, before you provide your
feedback.

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:32.840
Fiona Carey: Okay. Hello, my name is Fiona
Carey. I am a patient representative from

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:38.390
the east of England. I think that what Tim
spoke about - Tim was in our group this afternoon

00:40:38.390 --> 00:40:44.690
- was a pretty fair reflection of the discussions
that happened during the day, necessarily

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:49.040
distilled from a discussion that had already
been distilled, because these discussions

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:53.460
are necessarily a bit rushed. You are trying
to cram a lot in, and by the time everybody

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:59.560
has got to know each other's first name, there
is not a lot of the day left. It is very difficult

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:01.550
to do this well.

00:41:01.550 --> 00:41:06.950
Tim restated the issues. I think all of the
board members have clearly listened. They

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:13.920
have clearly taken notes, and they are reasonably
bright people, I think. They have recounted

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:20.920
the issues. I think what I would like to hear
next is clearer statements about what is being

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:32.860
committed to. We know how to do this. It is
about SMART objectives that are measurable.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:42.650
We are talking about a time limit, a delivery
date and being fairly clear and specific about

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:48.990
what it is we are going to deliver. I think
this is a very open door. We have had a great

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:53.740
day here. I should not go on. It has been
a tremendous day, and it has been very encouraging,

00:41:53.740 --> 00:41:58.880
really. It is great that the board are here.
However, as we said earlier, this is just

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:03.090
the start. It is going to be difficult to
do this. We are going to call you to account,

00:42:03.090 --> 00:42:09.630
because you invited us to, and we really intend
to make that serious. We will be back, and

00:42:09.630 --> 00:42:14.820
we will have questions to ask.

00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:21.110
Simon Burrell: Fiona, thank you. I am not
going to try and follow that. Emmerson, just

00:42:21.110 --> 00:42:24.660
say who you are and where you are from before
you reflect.

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:29.270
Emmerson Walgrove: Emmerson Walgrove from
Bradford Healthwatch and Chair of Bevan Health

00:42:29.270 --> 00:42:36.270
Patient Participation Group. Bruce did reflect
what was discussed in our discussion of mental

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:44.180
health, but I did not hear any solid commitments
on that. Are we going to have a commitment

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:51.310
that all GPs are educated and trained on mental
health and how to treat people? What about

00:42:52.300 --> 00:42:59.300
the commitment about getting education into
schools? It is about the prevention, not the

00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:07.710
reaction. It is about educating in primary
schools and getting our teaching staff educated

00:43:07.860 --> 00:43:14.860
about mental health. I did not hear a commitment
that service users are going to be respected

00:43:17.960 --> 00:43:24.790
across the NHS. There is no clear definition
in the NHS constitution about mental health

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:30.830
service users across the board being respected,
but it is clear about all NHS users. There

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:37.520
was no commitment to more funding of what
I - and, I think, most people - regard as

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:44.520
a Cinderella service across the country for
mental health. I did not hear a commitment

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:54.150
about more emphasis, but a fair emphasis.
What we have is a disparity of fairness across

00:43:55.790 --> 00:44:02.790
mental health about including in your five-year
plan clear information about mental health.

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:13.720
Simon Burrell: Thank you. That clearly resonates
with the room. We are only hearing from four

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:24.040
of the groups, because of failures largely
on my part. I did not manage to get the right

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:29.110
citizen up here. Ian, you are getting a slightly
easy ride, but we will make sure that that

00:44:29.110 --> 00:44:35.290
group gets a chance to come back to you, and
we will make sure that goes up on the NHS

00:44:35.290 --> 00:44:36.750
Citizen blog.

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:43.750
Cat: I am Cat, I am from Hertfordshire. I
have used mental health services for about

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:53.410
the last decade or so. One of those is as
a mental health service user. Having used

00:44:57.980 --> 00:45:03.670
services for about a decade, I have kind of
seen a lot. I also have worked for the NHS,

00:45:03.670 --> 00:45:10.670
and I now work with the NHS for my organisation.

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:21.750
So, for me, one of the things that I am really
happy about is that Jane really did get what

00:45:30.820 --> 00:45:36.940
was happening in the room. She really sat
down and listened to young people, even when

00:45:36.940 --> 00:45:41.240
they were saying pretty distressing things
about pretty bad experiences. Also, Jane,

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:46.100
I liked that you say something that was really
representative of young people, which is the

00:45:46.100 --> 00:45:51.150
side that we do not see. A lot of the time,
I think, young people get tarred with the

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:55.760
same brush as being apathetic. They are not;
they are disillusioned. They actually want

00:45:55.760 --> 00:46:00.400
someone to reach out to them, and today they
were actually given a space where they could

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:04.590
talk about the issues that are important to
them, and they actually had the ear of people

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:11.590
like Jane and Simon Stevens. One young person
in particular that I spoke to had no idea

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:16.310
it was Simon Stevens whose ear he was chewing.

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:20.940
What is really great is that this has been
an experience for everyone. I think everyone

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:26.080
has learned something, and I really hope that
actually we do get commitments for children

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:31.380
and young people, who too often are not seen
as politically important enough, because they

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:36.250
do not have a vote, or are told that they
do not have enough experience to understand

00:46:36.250 --> 00:46:40.150
what they are talking about. Actually, if
you have a condition, you are an expert by

00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:45.920
experience. Thank you, Jane, and thank you,
everyone, for coming.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:52.920
Olivia Butterworth: Cat, thank you. There
is a bit of a push back there that you have

00:46:57.140 --> 00:47:03.390
really heard and really understood, but we
are perhaps a little bit wishy-washy in our

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:05.970
commitments. Is that fair?

00:47:05.970 --> 00:47:07.110
Emmerson Walgrove: Can I say yes?

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:10.230
Olivia Butterworth: Okay, so just to put board
members on the spot, I wonder if anybody wants

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:15.410
to come back and say, okay, we are going to
go away and think about that. Tim?

00:47:15.410 --> 00:47:22.410
Tim Kelsey: I just want to say how impressed
I am. You have thanked us for listening, but

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:34.400
to be honest I am really quite moved by the
extraordinary way in which everyone has been

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:41.230
so honest and open. We are all in these jobs
because we care about making this health service

00:47:41.230 --> 00:47:46.910
- our health service - work better. We have
all individually - in this group, and everyone

00:47:46.910 --> 00:47:51.300
in the room - had good and bad experiences
of it. I feel really strongly that we have

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:56.190
a collective opportunity to do something really
different, and there are all sorts of reasons

00:47:56.190 --> 00:48:00.320
that we know about why we have to do that
quite urgently.

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:04.750
We will come back. I cannot speak for everybody,
but I am pretty confident we will come back

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:10.650
with real commitments that are really measurable
and absolutely stack up against what we said

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:13.620
we were going to do. If we are being wishy-washy,
it is not for lack of commitment. It is just

00:48:13.620 --> 00:48:20.620
because we need to spend some time really
thinking through how we can make the best

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:21.960
efforts.

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:28.960
Ian Dodge: Can I add to Tim's comments? In
the 20 years I have been working in health,

00:48:30.750 --> 00:48:37.090
I have seen lots of different commitments
given around tackling lots of the issues that

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:43.550
we are raising, and people who have made those
commitments have fervently wanted to make

00:48:43.550 --> 00:48:50.180
the NHS even better. If we have not succeeded,
it is because some of this is really difficult

00:48:50.180 --> 00:48:57.180
to do. We do not actually know how to crack
some of these issues. If we were to invent,

00:48:57.880 --> 00:49:04.880
here in this conversation, a set of new policies,
strategies and actions that said this is what

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:11.370
we are going to do, we would get it wrong.
That is one of the problems: how do we actually

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:18.370
design some of the answers with communities
and with patients? It is only by trying to

00:49:19.070 --> 00:49:24.500
understand better why things that we have
said would happen in the past have not happened

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:27.830
that we are actually likely to make some progress.

00:49:27.830 --> 00:49:32.760
The other point I wanted to pick up on is
that of course there are loads of challenges

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:38.350
and problems that we need to try and address.
However, equally, if you look at any international

00:49:38.350 --> 00:49:43.580
studies - and I am particularly thinking of
the work of the Commonwealth Fund - by international

00:49:43.580 --> 00:49:50.220
standards the NHS scores better than healthcare
in the United States and in many other countries,

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:51.450
and I think we should be very proud of it.

00:49:51.450 --> 00:49:58.450
Speaker: Why do you not stop the cuts and
privatisation? [Inaudible] commissioning.

00:50:00.750 --> 00:50:07.750
You are part of that process.

00:50:08.710 --> 00:50:12.820
Olivia Butterworth: Can we try and keep the
conversation going?

00:50:12.820 --> 00:50:18.660
Bruce Keogh: Can I please just come back to
the challenge about commitments on mental

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:25.660
health? It would be utterly dishonest of me
to walk out of one room, having heard a bunch

00:50:26.010 --> 00:50:32.660
of things which people are thinking about,
and then to stand up here a few minutes later

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:39.250
and commit to increasing funding, forcing
GPs to behave in a different way, and so on

00:50:39.250 --> 00:50:44.230
and so forth. I would have been absolutely
open to the criticism of meaningless promises.

00:50:44.230 --> 00:50:51.230
The job of this board is to listen today,
to go back and reflect in a proper way, and

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:58.920
then to determine exactly what we are going
to do and how we are going to do it. That

00:50:58.920 --> 00:51:01.560
is how we will get our credibility, not by
making meaningless immediate promises.

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:08.560
Speaker: What is dishonest is the [inaudible]
system. That is dishonest. That needs investigating.

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:14.140
Olivia Butterworth: Okay, so we have been
trying to have some respectful conversations,

00:51:14.140 --> 00:51:17.180
and we are going to come to questions from
the floor at the end of the session. If we

00:51:17.180 --> 00:51:18.710
could -
Speaker: - services. Why is that group not

00:51:18.710 --> 00:51:21.320
represented on the stage?

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:28.180
Olivia Butterworth: Because, from a facilitation
point of view, we simply did not have somebody

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:28.430
that -
Speaker: Is it because we asked the difficult

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:28.570
questions?

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:30.690
Olivia Butterworth: No, it is not because
you asked the difficult questions.

00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:32.970
Speaker: I think a lot of people in here think
it is because we said -

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:35.420
Olivia Butterworth: Okay. You cannot actually
be heard by the whole room. You cannot be

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:39.720
heard by the webcast -
Speaker: - off the stage.

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:46.720
Olivia Butterworth: Okay, so we are going
to wrap up this piece now, and I am going

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:55.730
to hand it back to Malcolm for the formal
part of the AGM. I think that both Malcolm

00:51:55.730 --> 00:52:00.590
and Simon will give reflections as well on
what they have heard today. A big thank you

00:52:00.590 --> 00:52:05.260
to our board members, who are in the audience
as well, who have participated and really

00:52:05.260 --> 00:52:12.260
listened today. A very big thank you to Emma,
who has left, Fiona, Emmerson and Cat for

00:52:12.890 --> 00:52:19.890
giving us really honest and insightful feedback,
and to everybody who participated throughout

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:33.430
the whole day today.

00:52:35.060 --> 00:52:42.060
Malcolm Grant: I turn now to the formal part
of the business, but before I do, I would

00:52:44.770 --> 00:52:51.770
like to repeat our grateful 

00:52:59.650 --> 00:53:06.520
thanks to Emma, Fiona, Emmerson and Cat. It
is not easy actually to come up on the stage

00:53:06.520 --> 00:53:13.520
and to present such profound comments with
honesty and integrity. Those are the sense

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:20.220
in which those views will be taken back by
my board.

00:53:20.220 --> 00:53:26.320
Later this evening, we will have an opportunity
for Q&amp;A, but I want to open the formal part

00:53:26.320 --> 00:53:32.870
of the business by saying first of all that
this is the occasion on which the board presents

00:53:32.870 --> 00:53:38.610
its annual report and accounts. That is a
document of which there is a summary in the

00:53:38.610 --> 00:53:43.870
foyer, and I hope people have been able to
pick it up. I am not going to take you in

00:53:43.870 --> 00:53:48.030
detail through it, because that would empty
the auditorium rather rapidly. However, I

00:53:48.030 --> 00:53:54.860
think it is appropriate that I draw attention
to some of the key messages that are there.

00:53:54.860 --> 00:54:01.860
First, to understand what NHS England is.
NHS England started life as the NHS Commissioning

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:10.560
board, and that captures the primacy of the
role that the organisation has. The Government's

00:54:11.060 --> 00:54:16.860
health reforms in 2012 were headed 'Liberating
the NHS'.

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:23.860
Speaker: [Inaudible].
Malcolm Grant: I would be very grateful for

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:32.960
an opportunity just to continue. The point
that I am making is that the board that will

00:54:32.960 --> 00:54:39.440
shortly be assembled on this stage has a central
role. Our responsibility is to share with

00:54:39.440 --> 00:54:45.890
the Secretary of State for Health the promotion
of a comprehensive health service of England,

00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:50.380
free at the point of need and available to
all the citizens -

00:54:50.380 --> 00:54:54.480
Speaker: [Inaudible 0:54:41].
Malcolm Grant: I am very grateful to you for

00:54:54.480 --> 00:54:58.290
comments, but please will you just allow me
to say what I would like to say? Thank you

00:54:58.290 --> 00:54:59.350
very much.

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:05.780
Now, the report outlines to you a number of
things that we have managed to do in our first

00:55:05.780 --> 00:55:12.780
year. Our job is to invest the budget of the
NHS to improve continuous improvement in the

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:20.140
health of England. What we do not do is run
hospitals. We do not run GP surgeries. Those

00:55:21.240 --> 00:55:28.240
are parts of a service that is a system that
is joined up, often imperfectly, but nonetheless

00:55:28.670 --> 00:55:35.670
an ecosystem that serves the entire country
of England. It is an ecosystem that is under

00:55:35.990 --> 00:55:40.880
enormous stress. Let me first of all point
to what some of those stresses are.

00:55:40.880 --> 00:55:46.910
We are now treating more patients in hospital
than ever. Do you know, we are now treating

00:55:46.910 --> 00:55:53.910
1.5 million people who are being referred
to treatment every month? Since 2010, our

00:55:55.070 --> 00:56:02.070
hospitals are now performing 2,000 more operations
every day. Our GPs are under increasing pressure.

00:56:03.170 --> 00:56:10.170
One million people see their GP every working
day. This is an extraordinary, complex system.

00:56:10.260 --> 00:56:17.260
Between 1999 and 2008, the average number
of consultations for GPs rose from 4.1 to

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:24.760
5.5 per patient per year. It is a consequence
of a number of socio-demographic trends, including

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:30.430
an aging population, which is excellent news,
but which makes new and quite complex demands

00:56:30.430 --> 00:56:36.960
on a health service. In parallel with that,
we have seen a very significant increase in

00:56:36.960 --> 00:56:43.510
attendances of patients at A&amp;E. The number
of attendances has risen by 2 million a year

00:56:43.510 --> 00:56:50.510
over the last 10 years to where it is now:
about 16 million attendances at A&amp;E every

00:56:50.650 --> 00:56:57.650
year. Emergency admissions, as a consequence
of attendance at A&amp;E or of blue-light arrival

00:56:58.060 --> 00:57:04.000
at hospital, have gone up year on year, and
over the past 10 years are up by 31%. By the

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:08.790
way, this is partly driven by an increase
in population, but much more by an increase

00:57:08.790 --> 00:57:13.810
in expectations and by an increase in demand
and changes in the burden of disease away

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:19.260
from those sorts of diseases and conditions
which brought about early death to those with

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:25.090
which people will live over time. An aging
population inevitably creates a different

00:57:25.090 --> 00:57:29.800
set of requirements and demands for the staff
of the NHS.

00:57:29.800 --> 00:57:36.800
At this point, I would like to make a comment
about those who serve us. The budget that

00:57:37.080 --> 00:57:43.900
we command ultimately employs some 1.3 million
people. It is an extraordinary workforce.

00:57:43.900 --> 00:57:49.010
Alongside it, by the way, is an extraordinary
voluntary workforce, some of which we heard

00:57:49.010 --> 00:57:54.590
about today - those people who provide care
in the communities and who support families,

00:57:54.590 --> 00:58:01.590
and whose work is very frequently not remunerated.
It is given out of love, out of duty, out

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:09.960
of support and, in many cases, sheer community-spiritedness,
much of which I heard this afternoon. One

00:58:10.790 --> 00:58:16.880
of the sessions that I attended was extremely
moving in understanding how to better connect

00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:20.710
care systems and provide that support.

00:58:20.710 --> 00:58:27.710
The situation, however, is very difficult.
Those increased demands on the provision of

00:58:29.490 --> 00:58:36.490
healthcare in England come at a time when
the pattern of demand has got out of alignment

00:58:36.660 --> 00:58:43.660
with the pattern of funding. I think the conditions
are very clear to everybody in this room.

00:58:44.440 --> 00:58:51.440
Since 1948, investment in the NHS has gone
up by about 3% to 4% per annum above inflation

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:58.900
- additional spending power of 3% to 4% per
annum above inflation. Since 2010, by the

00:59:30.280 --> 00:59:37.050
way, the pattern has changed. As a consequence
of the global financial crisis in 2008, funding

00:59:37.050 --> 00:59:44.050
for the NHS has been held at little over inflation.
At one level, you may say that is quite shocking

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:51.780
given that increase in demand, but if you
look at the increase and decrease in investment

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:57.710
in public services, you find that the NHS
has been protected. A number of other public

00:59:57.710 --> 01:00:03.700
services, including local government, have
not been protected.

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:05.530
Speaker: [Inaudible].

01:00:05.530 --> 01:00:12.530
Malcolm Grant: Please can I just give the
basics? Local government has experienced significant

01:00:13.330 --> 01:00:18.310
reductions in expenditure. That does not have
a beneficial impact in the relationship between

01:00:18.310 --> 01:00:24.400
the NHS and social care, which is something
that we need to turn our attention to. I am

01:00:24.400 --> 01:00:31.400
not saying that the NHS is in dire crisis,
and one of the reasons it is not is the extraordinary

01:00:31.740 --> 01:00:36.950
workforce to whom, publicly this evening,
I wish to express our enormous gratitude.

01:00:36.950 --> 01:00:43.950
All of us thank you. We also know that the
coming years are going to be difficult. In

01:00:49.740 --> 01:00:55.500
a few weeks' time, NHS England will be publishing
a five-year forward view of what we think

01:00:55.500 --> 01:01:01.300
the main principles are around which the NHS
can be regrouped and re-transformed for the

01:01:01.300 --> 01:01:07.760
future to provide the high quality of service
that we have heard our users require.

01:01:07.760 --> 01:01:13.900
Let me conclude by doing two things. One is
to thank you for joining us tonight. Another

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:19.360
one is to thank everyone on the staff of NHS
England for what they have done over the past

01:01:19.360 --> 01:01:25.410
year. It has been an extraordinary performance,
not least to set up a brand new organisation

01:01:25.410 --> 01:01:32.410
from scratch, to bring into play an entire
new model of funding the NHS, and to work

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:37.890
with such outstanding people. I would particularly
like to express my thanks to those who allowed

01:01:37.890 --> 01:01:44.890
us to produce our annual report on time and
to budget. In a moment, I will invite Paul

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:50.280
Baumann, our Financial Officer, to talk to
the figures, but first I would very much like

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:55.640
you, please, to welcome our Chief Executive,
Simon Stevens, who will talk about today and

01:01:55.640 --> 01:01:57.880
the future.

01:01:57.880 --> 01:02:04.880
Chief Executive's Report
Simon Stevens, Chief Executive, NHS England:

01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:24.480
I am going to keep it short and, hopefully,
sweet, because I get the impression that the

01:02:25.600 --> 01:02:31.240
woman here is keen to get stuck into the questions
and answers, and I am keen to do that as well.

01:02:31.240 --> 01:02:34.800
I know that there is a group over here who
also would like us to have a good old debate

01:02:34.800 --> 01:02:39.000
about the future of the NHS, which suits me
just fine. I am just going to say three things.

01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:44.020
High quality of care: First of all, those
who say that the NHS cannot make it, that

01:02:44.020 --> 01:02:48.240
it is going down the pan, and that we are
running it down deliberately, are wrong. That

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:54.580
is my state of assessment of the National
Health Service. The reason they are wrong

01:02:54.580 --> 01:02:59.800
is that actually the quality of care, despite
the incredibly difficult circumstances that

01:02:59.800 --> 01:03:04.900
we are having to face, is actually incredibly
high. That is what patients and the public

01:03:04.900 --> 01:03:09.640
say in this country. That is why they want
to defend and secure and build on the National

01:03:09.640 --> 01:03:14.630
Health Service, rather than canning it.

01:03:14.630 --> 01:03:19.310
The facts are these. Yes, this is the longest
period of budget austerity that we have ever

01:03:19.310 --> 01:03:25.080
seen for the National Health Service. We are
in year five of, effectively, a flat budget.

01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:30.710
Yes, that means that there are incredible
pressures on our staff - on GPs, on nurses

01:03:30.710 --> 01:03:35.970
and on front line employees, many of whom
have had to make huge sacrifice in terms of

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:40.780
the pay constraint that they have had. But
the fact is that, if you cast your mind back

01:03:40.780 --> 01:03:47.780
to what the NHS was like ten years ago, you
were waiting 18 months for a hip operation

01:03:48.490 --> 01:03:55.420
or a cataract. Now, most people are waiting
less than 18 weeks. The average wait for an

01:03:55.420 --> 01:03:56.820
operation is ten weeks.

01:03:56.820 --> 01:03:58.580
Speaker: That is because the funding increased.

01:03:58.580 --> 01:04:02.250
Simon Stevens: It is because the funding increased.
You are quite right. If I had my card, I would

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:07.390
be putting the Yes up back to you right now.
That proves the lie that the NHS cannot use

01:04:07.390 --> 01:04:14.390
investment to improve. It can, it has, and
in future it will.

01:04:16.770 --> 01:04:23.770
If you think back to the likelihood of surviving
in the 1970s if you had a heart attack, the

01:04:25.020 --> 01:04:32.020
fact is that one in four people would have
died. 19 out of 20 people now survive. If

01:04:33.050 --> 01:04:38.330
you think back to the 1970s, what were your
chances of surviving cancer? One in four people

01:04:38.330 --> 01:04:44.450
made it through the decade. One in two make
it through now. The fact is that the NHS is

01:04:44.450 --> 01:04:49.240
a model that works. Quality of care is very
high, and that is what the people that we

01:04:49.240 --> 01:04:50.510
serve uniformly report.

01:04:50.510 --> 01:04:55.650
Improvements needed: That is the first of
my three points. The second, however, is that

01:04:55.650 --> 01:04:59.390
all the conversations that we have been having
this afternoon, and all the discussions that

01:04:59.390 --> 01:05:06.360
we have right around the country, demonstrate
that what is great about the NHS cannot excuse

01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:12.560
what needs to change about the NHS. If you
had been in the conversation that I was in

01:05:12.560 --> 01:05:17.970
this afternoon, with people who have had often
some pretty dysfunctional experiences of our

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:22.720
mental health services, or if you had been
in the conversation that I and some of my

01:05:22.720 --> 01:05:26.260
colleagues were in with a group of people
with learning disabilities a couple of weeks

01:05:26.260 --> 01:05:33.260
ago, talking about how they were effectively
controlled and manipulated by like-it-or-lump-it

01:05:33.360 --> 01:05:38.430
health services, then you would recognise
that we need to improve. If you spent time,

01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:43.650
as I did in my first week after taking up
this job, with families and relatives in Mid

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:48.750
Staffordshire who have been on the receiving
end of poor-quality care - let us call a spade

01:05:48.750 --> 01:05:51.110
a spade - you would know that we need to improve.

01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:54.280
Speaker: [Crosstalk]. That is a soundbite.

01:05:54.280 --> 01:06:00.990
Simon Stevens: Well, it is actually the truth.
It is not just a soundbite, it is the result

01:06:00.990 --> 01:06:05.850
of two public inquiries and Sir Robert Francis's
report. Let the facts speak for the facts.

01:06:05.850 --> 01:06:07.640
Speaker: [Crosstalk] services go.

01:06:07.640 --> 01:06:14.640
Simon Stevens: Quite right, so -
Speaker: That group of people, just shut up.

01:06:20.300 --> 01:06:27.300
Simon Stevens: So -
Speaker: If you want to do the questions and

01:06:34.070 --> 01:06:35.570
answers, we will listen really politely to
you.

01:06:35.570 --> 01:06:37.160
Simon Stevens: Okay. Thank you for that corrective.

01:06:37.160 --> 01:06:40.070
Barriers and divisions; The third and final
point I want to make, in addition to the fact

01:06:40.070 --> 01:06:44.730
that quality of care is high despite all the
difficulties and the fact that that does not

01:06:44.730 --> 01:06:49.270
mean we should be complacent about the status
quo, is that as we look out over the next

01:06:49.270 --> 01:06:54.550
five years and beyond, we are going to have
to do things quite differently to overcome

01:06:54.550 --> 01:07:01.550
a number of the historic barriers, gaps and
false divisions that exist across the National

01:07:02.370 --> 01:07:05.520
Health Service. I think there are at least
six of them.

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:06.280
Physical health services versus mental health
services

01:07:06.280 --> 01:07:12.720
The first is the artificial divide between
general physical health services and mental

01:07:12.720 --> 01:07:18.080
health services. The question that came up
in the panel just now was what we are actually

01:07:18.080 --> 01:07:22.180
going to do about it. Well, here is one thing
that we are actually going to do about it

01:07:22.180 --> 01:07:29.180
as NHS England. For the first time ever, 25
years after waiting time standards were introduced

01:07:30.220 --> 01:07:36.220
for hip surgery or elective surgery in physical
health services, we will introduce mental

01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:42.680
health access standards from next year. It
will just be a start, and we will make progress,

01:07:42.680 --> 01:07:45.830
but that is part of getting equality of esteem.

01:07:45.830 --> 01:07:47.860
Community services versus hospital services

01:07:47.860 --> 01:07:52.050
There is an artificial divide in this country,
which is not just since 1948, but in fact

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:58.490
goes back way before that, between primary
care and hospital care - between general practice,

01:07:58.490 --> 01:08:03.410
community services and hospital services.
The GPs are, in many cases, worked off their

01:08:03.410 --> 01:08:08.290
feet. Patients are often passed pillar to
post between GPs, community services, A&amp;E

01:08:08.290 --> 01:08:14.290
departments and other parts of the system.
That needs to change. Three Friday nights

01:08:14.290 --> 01:08:20.290
ago, I was at the out-of-hours urgent care
service serving people in Barking, Redbridge

01:08:20.290 --> 01:08:24.859
and Waltham Forest, listening to some of those
conversations and calls with patients. It

01:08:24.859 --> 01:08:29.989
was pretty obvious that people were having
to try and navigate through completely confusing

01:08:29.989 --> 01:08:35.600
Heath Robinson-style systems that are in our
gift to change. So that is the second division:

01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:38.230
primary/secondary care. We have got to overcome
that.

01:08:38.230 --> 01:08:39.380
Health care versus social care

01:08:39.380 --> 01:08:45.549
The third is the historic split that we have
had in this country between health and social

01:08:45.549 --> 01:08:50.139
care. There are no easy answers as to how
we do this, particularly when it comes to

01:08:50.139 --> 01:08:54.659
funding, but the reality is that, seen from
the point of view of many of the heaviest

01:08:54.659 --> 01:08:59.759
users of our services, this now is not just
a distinction without a difference. It is

01:08:59.759 --> 01:09:04.580
a distinction which is actually undermining
the very ability of people to live the lives

01:09:04.580 --> 01:09:05.620
that they want to lead.

01:09:05.620 --> 01:09:06.639
Prevention versus treatment

01:09:06.639 --> 01:09:11.469
The fourth division that we have to overcome
is the difference between a focus purely on

01:09:11.469 --> 01:09:18.009
prevention, and the treatment consequences
of this. I was speaking yesterday at the conference

01:09:18.009 --> 01:09:23.210
of Public Health England about whether we
are just going to sleepwalk into the future

01:09:23.210 --> 01:09:29.480
of a nation where, for example, we ignore
wellbeing but also have an obesity epidemic

01:09:29.480 --> 01:09:35.949
that is on the rise. One in five of our children
now are obese at secondary school. We are

01:09:35.949 --> 01:09:42.949
on a track to lost lives, preventable illness
and billions of pounds in extra healthcare

01:09:45.150 --> 01:09:49.349
costs that actually weaken rather than strengthen
what it is that we are all trying to do. If

01:09:49.349 --> 01:09:52.429
we are going to get serious about that, it
is not just things the NHS can do. That is

01:09:52.429 --> 01:09:57.260
going to take a wider set of changes across
society, in my opinion. It is going to require

01:09:57.260 --> 01:10:02.690
more power for local councils. It is going
to require getting a little heavy with some

01:10:02.690 --> 01:10:08.969
of the junk food that is shoved down people's
throats. It is going to call for some frank

01:10:08.969 --> 01:10:14.940
conversations about the way in which we allow
people to make decisions about financial incentives

01:10:14.940 --> 01:10:18.960
and fiscal incentives, the way in which different
parts of the food chain are taxed, and so

01:10:18.960 --> 01:10:22.909
on. These are all difficult national conversations
we have to have, and I think the NHS has to

01:10:22.909 --> 01:10:26.980
have a voice in those conversations, rather
than just assuming that it is somebody else's

01:10:26.980 --> 01:10:27.260
business.

01:10:27.260 --> 01:10:28.380
Care provider versus employer

01:10:28.380 --> 01:10:34.670
I think we have a fifth set of changes we
have to think about, which is that the NHS,

01:10:34.670 --> 01:10:41.249
as well as being a care provider, is also
a massive employer. We know that for the 1.3

01:10:41.249 --> 01:10:46.449
million people that we are employing, whereas
actually the NHS could set a good example

01:10:46.449 --> 01:10:52.280
of a healthy workplace and a healthy workforce,
often we are the worst offenders when it comes

01:10:52.280 --> 01:10:59.280
to that. It does not just show up in the provision
of hospital food or various other things.

01:10:59.679 --> 01:11:05.179
It is the fact that whereas, for example,
three quarters of hospitals say that they

01:11:05.179 --> 01:11:12.179
provide support for quitting smoking to staff,
only a third do the same in terms of exercise

01:11:12.300 --> 01:11:17.619
or weight management or other things to help
our own staff in supported environments. We

01:11:17.619 --> 01:11:22.059
have got to change that. As part of that,
frankly we have got to be better as the NHS,

01:11:22.059 --> 01:11:29.059
soup to nuts, representing the communities
that we serve. We have too many leadership

01:11:29.510 --> 01:11:35.179
cadres in the NHS - too many boards of hospitals
and providers - that are not representative

01:11:35.179 --> 01:11:40.030
of the communities they serve. We have seen
particularly the exclusion of black and minority

01:11:40.030 --> 01:11:45.420
ethnic communities from the leadership of
many of our hospitals. That is bad for patients.

01:11:45.420 --> 01:11:50.940
It is bad for the NHS. It is ultimately the
root of where we fail to listen. We have got

01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:52.039
to make that change as well.

01:11:52.039 --> 01:11:53.179
Closed system versus community engagement

01:11:53.179 --> 01:11:56.159
The last of the changes that I think we have
to make as we look out is that we have got

01:11:56.159 --> 01:12:01.739
to stop seeing the NHS as a sealed factory
in some way, and recognise that it is embedded

01:12:01.739 --> 01:12:08.150
in the communities that it serves. That means
supporting, for example, the 5.5 million carers

01:12:08.150 --> 01:12:13.110
in this country. It means recognising we need
a completely changed relationship with volunteers.

01:12:13.110 --> 01:12:17.710
That is about a profound shift in power, and
I think we are just at the very early stages

01:12:17.710 --> 01:12:22.019
of thinking through what it would actually
mean. It is going to require some difficult,

01:12:22.019 --> 01:12:28.369
honest, sometimes confrontational but hopefully
nevertheless authentic conversations, of which

01:12:28.369 --> 01:12:29.840
tonight is just the beginning.

01:12:29.840 --> 01:12:34.530
I am proud to be here tonight, because in
this country we have a health service of the

01:12:34.530 --> 01:12:38.570
people, by the people and for the people,
and that is certainly what I am committed

01:12:38.570 --> 01:12:40.659
to sustaining. Thanks very much.

01:12:40.659 --> 01:12:47.659
Chief Financial Officer's Report
Paul Baumann, Chief Financial Officer, NHS

01:12:50.409 --> 01:12:56.699
England: Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.
The good news is I am a singer, so I can probably

01:12:56.699 --> 01:13:01.690
project my voice reasonably well. The bad
news is I am a singer with a very bad cold,

01:13:01.690 --> 01:13:04.480
so I probably cannot project it very clearly.

01:13:04.480 --> 01:13:07.619
I am going to try and keep my comments very
brief, because I am conscious I stand between

01:13:07.619 --> 01:13:12.159
a visionary statement and a debate on its
consequences. I do need, very briefly, to

01:13:12.159 --> 01:13:17.659
take you through the financial performance
of the NHS commissioning system for last year

01:13:17.659 --> 01:13:21.929
as part of the formalities of the AGM, and
that is what I will do in the next couple

01:13:21.929 --> 01:13:22.190
of minutes.

01:13:22.190 --> 01:13:27.469
212 new organisations: A year is a very long
time in the NHS. This time last year at the

01:13:27.469 --> 01:13:32.300
AGM, I reported on the financial performance
of a £60 million organisation with all of

01:13:32.300 --> 01:13:39.099
45 permanent staff and virtually no operational
responsibilities whatsoever. This year, I

01:13:39.099 --> 01:13:42.260
have got a couple of minutes and a couple
of charts to describe the financial affairs

01:13:42.260 --> 01:13:49.260
of a complex £96 billion group of 212 interdependent
organisations, employing the best part of

01:13:49.780 --> 01:13:56.780
28,000 people, created overnight on 1st April
2013, probably as the biggest start-up in

01:13:57.510 --> 01:13:59.760
British history.

01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:04.909
In my presentation last year, I spoke about
the herculean task ahead of us at that time

01:14:04.909 --> 01:14:10.519
of making sure that all of those 212 organisations
maintained financial control during their

01:14:10.519 --> 01:14:15.650
first year of operation and completed high-quality
accounts in line with what looked, to many,

01:14:15.650 --> 01:14:21.099
like a virtually impossible and challenging
timetable. Before turning to the numbers themselves,

01:14:21.099 --> 01:14:26.999
I want to put on record my deep appreciation
of the really herculean efforts and sheer

01:14:26.999 --> 01:14:32.539
professional skill which went in on the part
of the CCG teams, the Commissioning Support

01:14:32.539 --> 01:14:39.329
Unit teams and, indeed, the NHS England teams
into producing that set of accounts - 212

01:14:39.329 --> 01:14:44.130
sets of high-quality accounts delivered on
time, in full, without significant last-minute

01:14:44.130 --> 01:14:48.999
adjustments and, slightly to my surprise,
with universally positive, true and fair views

01:14:48.999 --> 01:14:53.769
from a set of auditors who are professionally
sceptical by nature and who certainly enjoyed

01:14:53.769 --> 01:15:00.329
that particular role last year.

01:15:00.329 --> 01:15:07.329
All of this 

01:15:11.800 --> 01:15:15.820
activity culminated in this report and accounts,
which Malcolm has mentioned. It was laid before

01:15:15.820 --> 01:15:20.949
Parliament on 22nd July. It is available from
the publication section of our website and,

01:15:20.949 --> 01:15:26.940
I am reliably informed, probably from all
good booksellers as well. At 191 pages long,

01:15:26.940 --> 01:15:31.389
it is probably not the most pithy tome you
will come across, but it does make a good

01:15:31.389 --> 01:15:34.329
read. And if it does not make a good read,
I have also discovered it is very good for

01:15:34.329 --> 01:15:40.619
furniture that does not quite sit evenly on
the ground, so do try that out if you prefer.

01:15:40.619 --> 01:15:46.369
I am also pleased to report that not only
was it done on time, but it fulfilled all

01:15:46.369 --> 01:15:50.960
six of the highly technical financial limits
and requirements put on us by the Department

01:15:50.960 --> 01:15:57.309
of Health and the Treasury. That gets accountants
very excited. For the rest of us, I suspect

01:15:57.309 --> 01:15:59.420
that, probably, the basic numbers will do.

01:15:59.420 --> 01:16:06.269
2013 performance: This is the 2013 performance.
As you can see from this chart, expenditure

01:16:06.269 --> 01:16:12.409
in 2013-14 was £279 million lower than planned.
Like everything in the NHS, that sounds like

01:16:12.409 --> 01:16:16.329
a horrendously big number, but it is actually
close to a point landing on a postage stamp

01:16:16.329 --> 01:16:23.329
at 0.3% of the planned spend of £94.7 billion.
Given the newness of the organisations concerned

01:16:23.920 --> 01:16:28.079
and the inherent risk associated with the
transition from the old to the new, I think

01:16:28.079 --> 01:16:34.469
most would agree that is a better outcome
than many commentators had expected.

01:16:34.469 --> 01:16:39.530
One of the key tasks for the finance teams
around the country during 2013-14 was to close

01:16:39.530 --> 01:16:44.510
down the balance sheets of PCTs and SHAs - a
massive and complex undertaking for which

01:16:44.510 --> 01:16:49.230
I would also like to express my thanks to
the finance teams concerned. The net result

01:16:49.230 --> 01:16:53.469
of this process was the so-called legacy you
can see at the bottom of that sheet - a positive

01:16:53.469 --> 01:16:58.809
adjustment to the closing balance sheets of
£133 million, equivalent to about 2.5% of

01:16:58.809 --> 01:17:04.389
the value estimated at the end of 2012-13,
which effectively comes through in our accounts

01:17:04.389 --> 01:17:06.409
as a one-off reduction in expenditure.

01:17:06.409 --> 01:17:10.150
Cumulative surplus: To complete the picture,
we have generally reported the performance

01:17:10.150 --> 01:17:14.659
of the NHS in terms of bottom-line surpluses
- the difference between the resources made

01:17:14.659 --> 01:17:18.849
available in our mandate and the expenditure
that I have just been reporting on. When I

01:17:18.849 --> 01:17:23.489
magically bring in the mandate funding, which,
fortunately, the Treasure does every year,

01:17:23.489 --> 01:17:29.260
you can see that our surplus excluding those
one-off legacy impacts amounts to £813 million,

01:17:29.260 --> 01:17:34.619
or just under 1% of our resources. It is also
important, though, just to remember that this

01:17:34.619 --> 01:17:39.780
is a cumulative surplus. It is not an in-year
number. It therefore includes all of the underspends

01:17:39.780 --> 01:17:44.059
brought forward from previous years, which
are given back to us in the mandate funding

01:17:44.059 --> 01:17:49.550
we receive from the Treasury. So actually
this £813 million surplus I have talked about

01:17:49.550 --> 01:17:54.619
at the end of 2013-14 is in fact a reduction
compared to the surplus we brought forward

01:17:54.619 --> 01:17:58.940
at the start of the year. In other words,
we have actually spent £371 million of the

01:17:58.940 --> 01:18:03.119
money brought forward from previous years
in addition to the money given to us in our

01:18:03.119 --> 01:18:08.420
recurrent allocation for last year. On the
positive side, that is less than the facility

01:18:08.420 --> 01:18:12.269
that we agreed with the Treasury and the Department
of Health, but for fairly obvious reasons

01:18:12.269 --> 01:18:16.929
we cannot continue to live off a declining
trend of prior year surpluses forever.

01:18:16.929 --> 01:18:22.170
Summary: In summary, our financial performance
was better than planned. We fulfilled all

01:18:22.170 --> 01:18:26.480
Treasury and Department of Health requirements,
but the tightening of the NHS finances is

01:18:26.480 --> 01:18:31.940
also becoming very apparent, both in our numbers,
as you can see, and in the well publicised

01:18:31.940 --> 01:18:37.449
challenges in the provider sector. It is for
that reason that I think we have to think

01:18:37.449 --> 01:18:43.769
of our financial journey as more of a marathon
than a one-year sprint. This happens to be

01:18:43.769 --> 01:18:47.860
the guy that put together the accounts I have
just presented to you. As you can see, I only

01:18:47.860 --> 01:18:52.579
recruit people who are slightly fitter than
I am. He is an ironman and marathon runner,

01:18:52.579 --> 01:18:56.749
but my point here is that we are all in this
for a financial marathon over the next few

01:18:56.749 --> 01:19:00.909
years. It is for that reason that it took
us significantly longer than in any year when

01:19:00.909 --> 01:19:06.539
I have been involved with the NHS, which is
a few but not many, to get our planning sorted

01:19:06.539 --> 01:19:10.409
out this year. It was much tougher than it
normally is. It is for that reason that, on

01:19:10.409 --> 01:19:14.880
a month-by-month basis, we are looking really
carefully at any sign of financial distress

01:19:14.880 --> 01:19:19.099
coming through in the system, and it is for
that reason that the five-year-view process

01:19:19.099 --> 01:19:23.599
that we have been talking about today, and
which came very strongly to the fore in Malcolm

01:19:23.599 --> 01:19:28.719
and Simon's speeches, is not just an important
thing to do for the future of the health service

01:19:28.719 --> 01:19:35.719
but increasingly urgent.

01:19:36.349 --> 01:19:43.349
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Paul.
On behalf of the whole board, I express our

01:19:45.289 --> 01:19:49.840
gratitude. There is so much that goes on in
the back rooms of organisations like this

01:19:49.840 --> 01:19:55.099
of such complexity and such herculean effort.
It is often not recognised and acknowledged

01:19:55.099 --> 01:20:02.099
in public. To get those annual reports and
accounts into Parliament by 22 July is unprecedented.

01:20:02.179 --> 01:20:08.820
Paul, I owe you and your team an enormous
debt of gratitude. Thank you so much for all

01:20:08.820 --> 01:20:13.260
that you have done.

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:18.769
Now we move to the questions and answers.
I am going to invite all the members of the

01:20:18.769 --> 01:20:24.489
NHS England board to join me on the stage.
I will introduce them, and we will proceed

01:20:24.489 --> 01:20:31.489
to the question-and-answer session.

01:20:35.159 --> 01:20:40.159
Q&amp;A

01:20:40.159 --> 01:20:47.159
Malcolm Grant: Ladies and gentlemen, what
a fine array. I will introduce all the members

01:21:09.949 --> 01:21:15.119
of the board in a moment, and then I will
outline how I would like to run the question

01:21:15.119 --> 01:21:20.969
and answer session. Please take this as testament
to the transparency of the operation of the

01:21:20.969 --> 01:21:25.550
board. I believe strongly that a board which
has the privilege, as we have, of serving

01:21:25.550 --> 01:21:30.510
the population in the way that we do should
be visible and accountable to you in public.

01:21:30.510 --> 01:21:34.900
I am going to introduce all of the members
of the board. Let me first of all explain

01:21:34.900 --> 01:21:38.989
that there are two types of members of the
board. I do not mean competent and incompetent;

01:21:38.989 --> 01:21:44.570
I mean there are two types of members of the
board. First, there are those who are our

01:21:44.570 --> 01:21:49.300
full-time employees - the so-called executive
members of the board, led by Simon Stevens

01:21:49.300 --> 01:21:55.559
as the Chief Executive. Then there are the
non-executive members, including myself - people

01:21:55.559 --> 01:22:02.449
who are not full-time employees of NHS England
but are, like on the board of a hospital trust,

01:22:02.449 --> 01:22:09.199
a major charity or a company, people from
outside who maintain a strict governance relationship

01:22:09.199 --> 01:22:15.030
with the executive team and hold them to account,
but also, in our case, work very closely as

01:22:15.030 --> 01:22:16.199
a team.

01:22:16.199 --> 01:22:21.559
Let me start on my far right with Noel Gordon,
who is a recent appointment to the board - a

01:22:21.559 --> 01:22:28.559
strategist with a lifetime of experience with
Accenture, the consultants. Lord Victor Adebowale

01:22:28.780 --> 01:22:34.869
of the House of Lords, but also the Chief
Executive of the charity Turning Point. Sir

01:22:34.869 --> 01:22:41.869
John Burn is an outstanding clinical geneticist
in the North East of England, and also a clinical

01:22:42.010 --> 01:22:49.010
entrepreneur, as well as being a major scientist
and professor there. Those three are non executive

01:22:49.429 --> 01:22:49.679
members.

01:22:49.530 --> 01:22:55.269
I turn to an executive member of the board,
Karen Wheeler, whose job title I love. She

01:22:55.269 --> 01:23:01.659
is the National Director for Transformation
and Operations. Those two merge together rather

01:23:01.659 --> 01:23:07.800
neatly. A non-executive director is Margaret
Casely-Hayford. Margaret has recently retired

01:23:07.800 --> 01:23:14.800
from an exceptional job which is of great
value to us: Secretary to the John Lewis Partnership.

01:23:15.239 --> 01:23:21.070
Understanding how a great organisation like
that works with its customers and with its

01:23:21.070 --> 01:23:26.369
staff has been invaluable to us. Ciaran Devane
is currently the Chief Executive of Macmillan

01:23:26.369 --> 01:23:32.380
Cancer Support, but shortly will take up a
new appointment at the Director General of

01:23:32.380 --> 01:23:33.780
the British Council.

01:23:33.780 --> 01:23:38.780
David Roberts, a recent appointment, has been,
until recently, the Deputy Chairman of the

01:23:38.780 --> 01:23:44.159
Lloyds Banking Group, and is now to be the
new chairman of Nationwide - somebody who

01:23:44.159 --> 01:23:49.989
can help us with those extraordinarily complex
figures that Paul Baumann has just been referring

01:23:49.989 --> 01:23:55.639
to. And Dame Moira Gibb, former Chief Executive
of the London Borough of Camden but with a

01:23:55.639 --> 01:24:02.429
lifetime career in social services and understanding
the relationship between social and health

01:24:02.429 --> 01:24:07.030
care. I should have said that those four are
non-executive directors.

01:24:07.030 --> 01:24:13.179
Ian Dodge, who has already been introduced,
is a new Director for Commissioning Strategy.

01:24:13.179 --> 01:24:20.179
Jane Cummings, our Chief Nurse for England,
and Barbara Hakin, whose role is Commissioning

01:24:20.889 --> 01:24:26.519
Operations, are again executive directors,
along with Sir Bruce Keogh, the nation's favourite

01:24:26.519 --> 01:24:33.519
doctor - sorry, the Medical Director of NHS
England, and Tim Kelsey, Director for Patients

01:24:33.900 --> 01:24:35.789
and Information.

01:24:35.789 --> 01:24:42.050
I think Simon Stevens needs no further introduction,
and nor does Paul Baumann. Of great importance

01:24:42.050 --> 01:24:49.050
to me is my Deputy Chairman, Ed Smith, a lifetime
of working in strategy and an extraordinary

01:24:49.820 --> 01:24:56.820
assistance to us all in devising the future
course of NHS England. So that is the team.

01:24:56.869 --> 01:25:02.070
Let me just suggest some rules of engagement,
because I think there is a lot of active interest,

01:25:02.070 --> 01:25:09.070
if I may say, in the room around the work
of NHS England. I suggest that rule one is

01:25:09.489 --> 01:25:16.489
respect. Anybody who listened to those voices
from the Citizens' Assembly will know what

01:25:16.619 --> 01:25:23.280
I mean. If you speak with respect, people
will listen and understand. This is not an

01:25:23.280 --> 01:25:28.639
occasion for barracking. Secondly, when I
invite people, please tell me your name and,

01:25:28.639 --> 01:25:33.710
if you want to, relevant background - if,
for example, you have experienced NHS care

01:25:33.710 --> 01:25:40.710
in some particular capacity. Thirdly, I suggest
that it is questions rather than speeches,

01:25:42.739 --> 01:25:46.769
because if you want answers, you will not
get them to speeches but you will get them

01:25:46.769 --> 01:25:51.469
to questions. Finally, what I would like to
do is take, say, three questions at once from

01:25:51.469 --> 01:25:58.139
different parts of the hall, and then invite
the appropriate members of the board to respond

01:25:58.139 --> 01:26:05.139
to those questions. I am seeing yes, which
I hope suggests agreement with my modus operandi.

01:26:06.469 --> 01:26:13.360
We have some roving microphones, and we are
about to kick off.

01:26:13.360 --> 01:26:20.360
I just need to make a note 
of the questions as they come in. There is
a gentleman there with his papers in the air.

01:26:26.940 --> 01:26:29.789
Shall we start with him, and then I will come
over in this direction.

01:26:29.789 --> 01:26:36.789
Dave Shepherd: I am a former member of a Community
Health Council and, until I resigned, an NHS

01:26:39.499 --> 01:26:46.499
Forum member. Two short questions. Can Dr
John Lister of Health Emergency, who says

01:26:49.489 --> 01:26:53.539
that the NHS exists in England and is worth
preserving, and Professor Allyson Pollock

01:26:53.539 --> 01:26:54.539
-

01:26:53.539 --> 01:26:59.139
Malcolm Grant: Sorry, I did not catch that.
Somebody said the NHS is what?

01:26:59.139 --> 01:27:06.019
Dave Shepherd: Dr John Lister says the NHS
exists in England and we should join any committee

01:27:06.019 --> 01:27:12.619
to help preserve its life. Professor Allyson
Pollock has written a book saying that the

01:27:12.619 --> 01:27:19.619
NHS does not exist any longer in England,
but does in Scotland and Wales. Can they both

01:27:19.900 --> 01:27:22.979
be right? What is the opinion of your finance
-

01:27:22.979 --> 01:27:26.130
Malcolm Grant: Okay. That is a really good
question to kick us off with.

01:27:26.130 --> 01:27:26.670
Dave Shepherd: Second question -

01:27:26.670 --> 01:27:28.130
Malcolm Grant: Oh, there is a second one.
Right.

01:27:28.130 --> 01:27:35.019
Dave Shepherd: I am actually also an unpaid
creditor of the NHS in respect of the compulsory

01:27:35.019 --> 01:27:41.420
privatised part of my blood contributions,
which I said should not go to private patients.

01:27:41.420 --> 01:27:48.300
They are given free to private hospitals and
patients. When may I expect the refund of

01:27:48.300 --> 01:27:55.300
the value of half a pint, not the nineteen
and a half pints, and the 32 years' interest?

01:27:58.670 --> 01:28:05.150
I do not mind setting off the expenditure
of the NHS on the biscuits and coffee and

01:28:05.150 --> 01:28:10.320
tea and orange juice that I consumed as a
contributor to the NHS blood bank.

01:28:10.320 --> 01:28:15.460
Malcolm Grant: Well, I am grateful for that.
That is a set-off which we appreciate. Let

01:28:15.460 --> 01:28:19.789
me take question two - gentleman right here
in the front row, if I may.

01:28:19.789 --> 01:28:24.190
Maurice Bernard: I am a retired consultant
psychiatrist and also a member of the NHS

01:28:24.190 --> 01:28:25.840
Consultants' Association. It was mentioned
from the stage that you want to listen to

01:28:25.840 --> 01:28:32.840
the public, and this document says the public
should be empowered, but the public do not

01:28:38.920 --> 01:28:45.920
want NHS services to be privatised. One of
the arguments for Scotland to become an independent

01:28:48.229 --> 01:28:55.229
country, which Alex Salmond has said, is 
to avoid the privatisation which has occurred
in England. They do not want it in Scotland.

01:29:06.170 --> 01:29:08.610
Malcolm Grant: Sorry, is there a question?

01:29:08.610 --> 01:29:15.610
Maurice Bernard: Well, the question is what
is going to be done about the conflict of

01:29:17.920 --> 01:29:23.650
interest between the private sector making
profit and providing a quality service?

01:29:23.650 --> 01:29:30.650
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much.
The gentleman holding up a yes or no sign.

01:29:33.710 --> 01:29:36.659
Speaker: Remember the gender balance, please.

01:29:36.659 --> 01:29:42.249
Malcolm Grant: Sorry, we have got a long way
to go this evening. Please relax. Time will

01:29:42.249 --> 01:29:42.630
come.

01:29:42.630 --> 01:29:49.630
Mark Boothroyd: I am a staff nurse in the
NHS. I am part of a coalition called Docs

01:29:50.309 --> 01:29:55.380
not Cops, and we are really concerned about
the impact of the Immigration Act on access

01:29:55.380 --> 01:30:02.360
to NHS services. Basically, we think it is
going to deprive migrants and other marginalised

01:30:02.360 --> 01:30:07.659
groups of access to the NHS and be the first
step in introducing charges into the NHS.

01:30:07.659 --> 01:30:12.989
How are you, as the board of NHS England,
going to make sure that no one who needs NHS

01:30:12.989 --> 01:30:17.989
treatment is deprived of access to it? How
are you going to stop NHS staff being complicit

01:30:17.989 --> 01:30:23.769
in depriving people of access, which is going
to be the end result of any kind of charging?

01:30:23.769 --> 01:30:28.519
We are getting reports already that people
are being turned away from GP services, that

01:30:28.519 --> 01:30:32.420
people are already being possibly charged
wrongly for services, and the Immigration

01:30:32.420 --> 01:30:34.639
Act and the charging is just going to make
that worse.

01:30:34.639 --> 01:30:41.059
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you for that question.
What I am going to do, having taken those

01:30:41.059 --> 01:30:46.610
three questions, I am going to invite Simon.
I think Simon - thank you very much - you

01:30:46.610 --> 01:30:50.699
may be able to run one or two of those together
because they speak to a particular agenda.

01:30:50.699 --> 01:30:56.659
Simon Stevens: So, on the first one, on David's
question 'Does the NHS still exist?' Well,

01:30:56.659 --> 01:31:01.409
yes, it does. It still exists in England,
and the 1.3 million people who work in it

01:31:01.409 --> 01:31:06.130
would be surprised to hear that they worked
for an organisation which does not now exist.

01:31:06.130 --> 01:31:10.639
So I think that is pretty self-evident; of
course the NHS exists. Should it continue

01:31:10.639 --> 01:31:15.829
to exist? In all of our views, most certainly,
and we obviously, no doubt, through further

01:31:15.829 --> 01:31:19.260
conversation tonight, will discuss in what
ways you think it should and what ways you

01:31:19.260 --> 01:31:19.880
think it should not.

01:31:19.880 --> 01:31:25.249
That comes on to the question at the front
here around how do you deal with the situation

01:31:25.249 --> 01:31:32.249
where you have got non-NHS providers, and
how do you deal with the quality concerns?

01:31:33.010 --> 01:31:39.289
The answer there, I think, is that everybody
providing services for NHS patients has to

01:31:39.289 --> 01:31:44.670
now go through the Care Quality Commission
independent quality inspectorate so that there

01:31:44.670 --> 01:31:48.979
is complete transparency as to how well they
are doing, and where they are no good, they

01:31:48.979 --> 01:31:51.199
get kicked out.

01:31:51.199 --> 01:31:58.199
In terms of the question on migrants, one
of the people who was here and I was speaking

01:31:58.420 --> 01:32:04.360
to earlier this afternoon in fact, was a guy
from Bolton who is an Afghanistani refugee.

01:32:04.360 --> 01:32:10.010
We were talking about the experience that
refugees have around the country, and I think

01:32:10.010 --> 01:32:14.449
you put your finger on a very important point,
not just about refugees but actually about

01:32:14.449 --> 01:32:20.090
what, in a patronising way, the NHS has traditionally
called 'hard to reach communities.' There

01:32:20.090 --> 01:32:24.269
are no hard to reach communities, there are
just hard to reach services. So I think we

01:32:24.269 --> 01:32:28.860
have actually got to turn the conversation
round and get much more engaged.

01:32:28.860 --> 01:32:33.300
I think Bruce was nodding vehemently when
you were making your point there, so maybe

01:32:33.300 --> 01:32:36.400
he wants to add to that? Or maybe he does
not.

01:32:36.400 --> 01:32:41.289
Malcolm Grant: No. I am worried about David's
blood; I hope we can come back to David's

01:32:41.289 --> 01:32:45.179
blood. Can I invite any other member of the
board if they have any response they would

01:32:45.179 --> 01:32:46.920
like to add to Simon's? No.

01:32:46.920 --> 01:32:51.369
In which case, let's romp on; let's take another
three. I know there is a gender complaint

01:32:51.369 --> 01:32:53.699
from over here. So let me please -

01:32:53.699 --> 01:33:00.699
Okay, let's take it in turn. You sir are a
man, not you. Please, madam in the front?

01:33:01.329 --> 01:33:04.159
Rohana Azam: My name is Rohana Azam.

01:33:04.159 --> 01:33:05.079
Malcolm Grant: Sahana?

01:33:05.079 --> 01:33:05.989
Rohana Azam: Rohana.

01:33:05.989 --> 01:33:06.909
Malcolm Grant: Rohana.

01:33:06.909 --> 01:33:12.679
Rohana Azam: Just a really quick question.
The public do not want the NHS sold off, so

01:33:12.679 --> 01:33:19.150
can the board give us some assurance that
they will stop selling our NHS off, please?

01:33:19.150 --> 01:33:20.369
Thank you.

01:33:20.369 --> 01:33:24.539
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Not you sir because you
are right next to her. I am going to jump

01:33:24.539 --> 01:33:31.539
around the room. That lady there with her
hand up. Yes, that is you. Please?

01:33:31.789 --> 01:33:38.789
Judith: Hi. I am Judith, and I am just an
ordinary person. I am American, although I

01:33:41.329 --> 01:33:48.329
have lived here half my life. I just wonder,
we understand that there is a trade deal on

01:33:49.019 --> 01:33:52.979
the table that will Americanise our services
here. As somebody who has experienced, for

01:33:52.979 --> 01:33:59.979
quite a long time, the American health system,
you do not want it here believe me.

01:34:08.869 --> 01:34:15.570
Malcolm Grant: Please, let Judith speak.

01:34:15.570 --> 01:34:22.570
Judith: The second part of that, this is the
question. Is there some way that we can avoid

01:34:28.760 --> 01:34:35.760
the consequences of a health service split
up and run purely for profit that we have

01:34:36.050 --> 01:34:41.929
in America? If you just look at it, for one
thing it does not work. It has tier after

01:34:41.929 --> 01:34:47.969
tier of people and organisations being ripped
off; the insurance companies, everything;

01:34:47.969 --> 01:34:51.019
the people who have to have the insurance.

01:34:51.019 --> 01:34:52.949
Malcolm Grant: So the question is, 'Is there
a way we can avoid that?'

01:34:52.949 --> 01:34:54.030
Judith: The question is -

01:34:54.030 --> 01:34:55.260
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you very much.

01:34:55.260 --> 01:34:56.380
Judith: Just please avoid it.

01:34:56.380 --> 01:34:59.499
Malcolm Grant: No. I am very grateful for
that. I am going to come into the central

01:34:59.499 --> 01:35:06.499
part of the room. John, right there, the lady
in the other row there. Please, yes?

01:35:06.739 --> 01:35:09.900
Pearl: Hello. I am also a -

01:35:09.900 --> 01:35:11.219
Sir Bruce Keogh: Sorry, your name?

01:35:11.219 --> 01:35:18.219
Pearl: My name is Pearl. I am also an American
who is now a British citizen for many, many

01:35:19.829 --> 01:35:26.829
years, and I also experienced both sides of
healthcare. So I am very, very, very concerned

01:35:32.369 --> 01:35:39.369
about CETA, ISDS and TTIP, and how the NHS
is not being excluded. So can NHS England

01:35:49.479 --> 01:35:56.479
have any influence upon our government to
exempt our NHS from the Transatlantic Free

01:35:59.300 --> 01:35:59.889
Trade Agreements?

01:35:59.889 --> 01:36:00.829
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

01:36:00.829 --> 01:36:02.400
Pearl: That is a question.

01:36:02.400 --> 01:36:04.909
Malcolm Grant: Right. Do you know there is
-

01:36:04.909 --> 01:36:11.909
Pearl: Then I have the second question, which
is, it seems to me an elephant in the room

01:36:17.349 --> 01:36:24.349
of all of these discussions, that there seems
to be a complete disconnect between the benefit

01:36:25.559 --> 01:36:32.559
system and the NHS. Can NHS England find a
way to influence the DWP to stop punishing

01:36:41.289 --> 01:36:48.289
and killing people with their Work Capability
Assessment and return responsibility for assessing

01:36:51.749 --> 01:36:58.749
people of ill health for benefit back to NHS
medical personnel? Thank you. That is two

01:37:01.150 --> 01:37:01.739
questions.

01:37:01.739 --> 01:37:08.739
Malcolm Grant: Okay. No, thank you very much.
I think there are two aspects to those three,

01:37:09.949 --> 01:37:16.010
four, questions. The first one is about selling
off the NHS. I am just going to ask the board

01:37:16.010 --> 01:37:21.659
'Is there anybody on the board who is in favour
of selling off the NHS?'

01:37:21.659 --> 01:37:24.880
Speaker: You are doing it already.

01:37:24.880 --> 01:37:30.829
Malcolm Grant: No. No, no. [Crosstalk] No.
Can we just be clear, we have an NHS which

01:37:30.829 --> 01:37:36.320
is largely funded from the public purse, which,
of course, is a sharp contrast with the US

01:37:36.320 --> 01:37:40.840
system. The other sharp contrast with the
US system is that we spend, from the public

01:37:40.840 --> 01:37:46.539
purse, about 8% of GDP. There is another private
and charitable contribution of about 1.6%

01:37:46.539 --> 01:37:53.539
of GDP; takes us up to about 9.6% of GDP in
England. In continental Europe, the proportion

01:37:55.130 --> 01:38:01.099
of GDP is more like 11%; it varies between
France, Germany and parts of Scandinavia.

01:38:01.099 --> 01:38:07.869
In the United States of America it is over
18% of GDP. One of the explanations for the

01:38:07.869 --> 01:38:12.780
difference between them is the rather fragmented
system of care in the US; the difference between

01:38:12.780 --> 01:38:18.579
those who provide care and - will you please
let me finish - those who provide health insurance,

01:38:18.579 --> 01:38:23.969
and also my own profession, which is the lawyers,
who succeed in exploiting the difference between

01:38:23.969 --> 01:38:30.199
the two with very large negligence claims.
Nobody that I know would import wholesale

01:38:30.199 --> 01:38:35.479
the system of US healthcare, and indeed everybody
I know in the US is struggling to find a way

01:38:35.479 --> 01:38:40.309
to bring it into line with something which
is more socially acceptable, which is a collectivised

01:38:40.309 --> 01:38:42.070
model that we have in this country.

01:38:42.070 --> 01:38:49.070
You asked also about TTIP and the international
trade deal. I have to confess that, of all

01:38:50.999 --> 01:38:57.179
the responsibilities the government has conferred
upon us, negotiating with other foreign states

01:38:57.179 --> 01:39:03.219
is not one of them. The TTIP question, I think,
has to be directed to the government, which

01:39:03.219 --> 01:39:09.059
has that responsibility; not to this board,
which has no responsibility for international

01:39:09.059 --> 01:39:10.269
trade.

01:39:10.269 --> 01:39:17.269
I think that the third question though, which
really does take me and capture my attention,

01:39:18.889 --> 01:39:24.969
is about the relationship between the benefit
system and the NHS, and the role of DWP. I

01:39:24.969 --> 01:39:30.539
am going to look and ask whether Simon might
be able to provide us with some further information

01:39:30.539 --> 01:39:30.940
on that.

01:39:30.940 --> 01:39:34.679
Simon Stevens: I think this is obviously one
of the areas of the welfare state that is

01:39:34.679 --> 01:39:41.679
not working well, and one piece of evidence
for that is that 300,000 people a year come

01:39:42.309 --> 01:39:47.639
out of employment and end up on benefits because
of ill health problems that are not being

01:39:47.639 --> 01:39:52.409
properly supported through the NHS or through
the kind of processes you are talking about.

01:39:52.409 --> 01:39:58.489
So I think one of the big offers that the
NHS can make to the country is that by doing

01:39:58.489 --> 01:40:04.989
a better job in terms of our mental health
services, our therapy services and other things,

01:40:04.989 --> 01:40:10.380
actually we will keep people healthier, we
will keep them at work and we will enable

01:40:10.380 --> 01:40:17.380
them not to end up on benefits. At that moment,
that is of no advantage to anybody. So one

01:40:17.479 --> 01:40:20.849
of the investments that is being made right
now is improving mental health services; people

01:40:20.849 --> 01:40:25.159
with anxiety and depression, and there is
this programme called IAPT that is doing that.

01:40:25.159 --> 01:40:29.459
There is a lot more we could be doing there,
and if we do I think that will be a win-win

01:40:29.459 --> 01:40:32.699
for people and also for the country as a whole.

01:40:32.699 --> 01:40:36.789
Malcolm Grant: Is there any other member of
the board would wish to add anything on those

01:40:36.789 --> 01:40:37.389
questions? No.

01:40:37.389 --> 01:40:42.539
Well then, let's move on to the next batch.
I am moving gradually back in this direction,

01:40:42.539 --> 01:40:49.539
but I am going to move perhaps to the middle
of the room. Madam, with the pink top there.

01:40:52.729 --> 01:40:53.630
Thank you very much.

01:40:53.630 --> 01:41:00.630
Kim Sanders-Fisher: Kim Sanders-Fisher. I
was trained in the US. Many years in American

01:41:01.229 --> 01:41:08.079
hospitals, in Jackson Memorial and at Johns
Hopkins, come to this country and I have gone

01:41:08.079 --> 01:41:15.079
straight to the NHS scrap heap. Why? I am
whistle-blower. I want to know when the NHS

01:41:16.729 --> 01:41:23.729
is going to stop squandering obscene amounts
of money protecting the negligent at the expense

01:41:24.260 --> 01:41:28.090
of conscientious whistle-blowers who are demonised
for life?

01:41:28.090 --> 01:41:33.880
Malcolm Grant: Right. That is a good question.
Thank you. We will certainly take that question

01:41:33.880 --> 01:41:38.790
on. Please could I take a second one? I am
anxious not to overlook people. Madam, there

01:41:38.790 --> 01:41:43.769
- sorry, there are two ladies behind each
other; the one in front is waving harder,

01:41:43.769 --> 01:41:47.479
so give it to the one behind her. No, no,
that will do fine. I will come back to you

01:41:47.479 --> 01:41:48.409
madam with the next one.

01:41:48.409 --> 01:41:50.789
Speaker: I hate to say it is another American
standing up.

01:41:50.789 --> 01:41:54.889
Malcolm Grant: Oh no, this is fantastic. This
is a global seminar.

01:41:54.889 --> 01:42:01.889
Speaker: I've been here 44 years. The reason
I love the UK is because of the NHS, and I

01:42:02.610 --> 01:42:09.610
go back to the States all the time and I say
'You do not have what we have over here.'

01:42:10.820 --> 01:42:11.559
Then I get so much heart problems in the USA
of people who cannot afford their health insurance,

01:42:11.559 --> 01:42:16.789
but that is not my question. My question is
directed to the Finance Officer, it is to

01:42:16.789 --> 01:42:20.199
do with PFI, which is crippling the hospitals
of paying back all these 30-year and 40-year

01:42:20.199 --> 01:42:25.409
contracts. On Radio 4 this morning they were
discussing that there are several NHS trusts

01:42:25.409 --> 01:42:30.949
who have renegotiated, they have paid off
their contracts so they do not have to pay

01:42:30.949 --> 01:42:37.949
all this outrageous interest for the new builds,
the new hospitals. Why can't the Finance Director

01:42:39.329 --> 01:42:45.900
or NHS England make it mandatory that every
Trust goes back and renegotiates to pay off

01:42:45.900 --> 01:42:47.229
their PFI contracts?

01:42:47.229 --> 01:42:52.260
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you very much for
that. Paul, in due course I will come to you

01:42:52.260 --> 01:42:57.019
on that. I did promise to the lady immediately
behind you for number three.

01:42:57.019 --> 01:43:04.019
Christine Mitchell: Christine Mitchell, Chelmsford.
This lady has just asked my question. My problem

01:43:05.190 --> 01:43:09.709
with the PFI is when I saw what it meant,
scam came to mind. I am sorry, the PFI is

01:43:09.709 --> 01:43:12.610
a Tony Blair scam and I want to know why it
has not been investigated? Hospitals are haemorrhaging

01:43:12.610 --> 01:43:19.469
money. There is also another point. Why do
we put off nurses and then hire agency nurses

01:43:19.469 --> 01:43:21.010
at a greater expense?

01:43:21.010 --> 01:43:25.239
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Can I take those questions?

01:43:25.239 --> 01:43:32.239
Christine Mitchell: Excuse me, just one more
thing. This is all to do with cutbacks: MRSA.

01:43:36.320 --> 01:43:43.320
If we are all carrying it around, how comes
it never manifests itself till it gets into

01:43:48.849 --> 01:43:55.849
a British hospital? Please do not tell me
it is everywhere. I am a retired coach driver

01:43:57.170 --> 01:43:59.659
and I have seen hospitals all over Europe,
very proud they have adopted a British idea,

01:43:59.659 --> 01:44:03.499
but they have perfected on it. Contrary to
your belief, we are lagging behind German

01:44:03.499 --> 01:44:08.499
hospitals, Spanish hospitals, French hospitals;
our service is lagging behind.

01:44:08.499 --> 01:44:13.280
Malcolm Grant: Right. Can I get an answer
in due course from our Chief Medical Officer

01:44:13.280 --> 01:44:13.929
and our Chief Nurse?

01:44:13.929 --> 01:44:20.459
Right, that was another three eventually.
I think the first one on whistle blowing,

01:44:20.459 --> 01:44:25.789
in his first week as Chief Executive, Simon
Stevens picked up the cudgels on whistle blowing;

01:44:25.789 --> 01:44:30.449
met a team of whistle-blowers and has been
working hard on that area. Simon, please can

01:44:30.449 --> 01:44:31.849
you take us forward on that?

01:44:31.849 --> 01:44:38.749
Simon Stevens: Yeah. The fact is that whistle-blowers
do the NHS a great favour. For too long whistle-blowers

01:44:38.749 --> 01:44:45.139
have been ignored and harassed, and that is
one of the reasons why we have asked Robert

01:44:45.139 --> 01:44:51.389
Francis to look at, with whistle-blowers,
what needs to change about the way in which,

01:44:51.389 --> 01:44:56.459
when frontline staff have got issues about
the quality of care or other things they see,

01:44:56.459 --> 01:45:02.869
they can raise those concerns and not get
trapped and snared in all kinds of employment

01:45:02.869 --> 01:45:08.380
related stuff which all too often gets thrown
their way. That is not to say that every whistle

01:45:08.380 --> 01:45:12.979
blower will always get it right, but it is
to say that whistle blowing is a public service.

01:45:12.979 --> 01:45:17.670
So Robert Francis is supposed to be producing
his recommendation on how to protect whistle

01:45:17.670 --> 01:45:22.349
blowers in a way that goes beyond the limited
protections that are in the Public Interest

01:45:22.349 --> 01:45:26.510
Disclosure Act 1999, and he is supposed to
be reporting in November.

01:45:26.510 --> 01:45:26.760
Christine Mitchell: I contributed to that.
I wanted to contribute to that.

01:45:26.539 --> 01:45:26.789
Malcolm Grant: Good. So thank you.

01:45:26.550 --> 01:45:26.800
Simon Stevens: Great.

01:45:26.559 --> 01:45:29.760
Christine Mitchell: It is one of three things
that I contributed to.

01:45:29.760 --> 01:45:36.760
Malcolm Grant: Yep, okay. Well, thank you.
I hope Simon's response is reassuring. I see

01:45:38.260 --> 01:45:41.159
a number of 'yes' signs up in the air.

01:45:41.159 --> 01:45:47.110
I wanted now to turn to PFI, the two questions
regarding PFI, and see whether Paul can help

01:45:47.110 --> 01:45:47.999
us on that?

01:45:47.999 --> 01:45:54.219
Paul Baumann: So there have been lots and
lots of reports about PFIs, as you will all

01:45:54.219 --> 01:45:59.289
know; I think it has been extensively made
transparent in terms of what the implications

01:45:59.289 --> 01:46:04.269
are. There are two main issues with PFI hospitals.
One of which is that, in some cases, the financial

01:46:04.269 --> 01:46:09.860
deals concerned are simply not, were not,
good value for money; the cost is too high.

01:46:09.860 --> 01:46:14.499
The other is, in some cases, we have got hospitals
which are much too highly spec ed and therefore

01:46:14.499 --> 01:46:16.769
there is a capacity issue in it.

01:46:16.769 --> 01:46:20.650
The fact of the matter though is that are
legally binding contracts which the government

01:46:20.650 --> 01:46:27.159
cannot simply rip up and move on from. Where
there is a possibility of buying them out

01:46:27.159 --> 01:46:31.280
with value for money, and in a couple of cases
I think that has been done, it will be done.

01:46:31.280 --> 01:46:34.769
Where you would have to give more money to
get out of the contracts than it costs to

01:46:34.769 --> 01:46:38.979
keep them going, self evidently we would not
contemplate doing that. I do not see any other

01:46:38.979 --> 01:46:39.979
options.

01:46:38.979 --> 01:46:39.229
Christine Mitchell: How can you stick to a
contract that is not legal in the first place?

01:46:38.979 --> 01:46:42.179
If you investigate further, the beginnings
of the PFI system, you will see it is a scam.

01:46:42.179 --> 01:46:49.179
Tony Blair is still raking off from it.

01:46:50.329 --> 01:46:57.329
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Tony Blair I am
not answering for, but I am going to call

01:46:58.420 --> 01:46:59.010
on Bruce.

01:46:59.010 --> 01:46:59.989
Speaker: And so was [inaudible] John Major.

01:46:59.989 --> 01:47:03.130
Malcolm Grant: Bruce, did you want to come
in?

01:47:03.130 --> 01:47:10.130
Bruce Keogh: Thank you for raising the issue
of MRSA. It was a major problem. We have made

01:47:13.280 --> 01:47:20.280
very, very significant inroads into it, and
we measure it really quite aggressively. There

01:47:20.749 --> 01:47:25.650
are many hospitals now which are reporting
long, long periods, over a year, without any

01:47:25.650 --> 01:47:27.309
case of MRSA.

01:47:27.309 --> 01:47:33.110
Let me just tell you what my real worry is.
My real worry is that globally there is only

01:47:33.110 --> 01:47:39.659
one new antibiotic in the pipeline, and that
around the world antibiotic resistance is

01:47:39.659 --> 01:47:45.530
building up. This is going to pose a major
threat to all healthcare systems unless we

01:47:45.530 --> 01:47:51.550
can grapple with it pretty quickly, and that
has become a major focus, if you like, of

01:47:51.550 --> 01:47:55.929
the Chief Medical Officer in the Department
of Health and is being taken very seriously

01:47:55.929 --> 01:47:58.900
by the World Health Organization and this
government. It is just something that we need

01:47:58.900 --> 01:48:02.459
to bear in mind because it could be a lot
worse than MRSA.

01:48:02.459 --> 01:48:06.729
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much Bruce.
There was question also about nursing, agency

01:48:06.729 --> 01:48:09.820
nurses, which, if I may, will ask Jane to
answer.

01:48:09.820 --> 01:48:16.749
Jane Cummings: Yeah. Thanks Malcolm. I agree,
I think, in the sense that agency nurses can

01:48:16.749 --> 01:48:22.809
be useful at times if they needed to be added
to a normal set of staffing, if you have got

01:48:22.809 --> 01:48:29.809
a particular issue or a particular patient
that needs additional care. However, I would

01:48:29.940 --> 01:48:35.690
express caution and concern if this was being
done on a regular basis, and it is something

01:48:35.690 --> 01:48:42.690
that we will look in more detail to define
and to report on, probably in more detail

01:48:43.239 --> 01:48:45.209
next year. So we are looking at it.

01:48:45.209 --> 01:48:49.139
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. I think Tim Kelsey
wants to have a word.

01:48:49.139 --> 01:48:53.130
Tim Kelsey: It was just really on that point
on whistle blowing, if I can go quickly back

01:48:53.130 --> 01:48:57.809
to it. I got into this job ultimately because
my mother, who is a GP, felt she had to blow

01:48:57.809 --> 01:49:04.300
the whistle on a dangerous doctor in the South
West. That is where my journey started. My

01:49:04.300 --> 01:49:08.959
response was not just to reflect, as everyone
has done, that it is vitally important we

01:49:08.959 --> 01:49:13.880
make it easier for individual members of staff
to blow the whistle, but equally that the

01:49:13.880 --> 01:49:18.190
service had to become massively more transparent
about the quality of care provided. One of

01:49:18.190 --> 01:49:23.349
the things I would like to just tell you is
how committed we are at NHS England to proper,

01:49:23.349 --> 01:49:26.880
real transparency of quality in healthcare,
and one of the things I think we should be

01:49:26.880 --> 01:49:31.769
proud of is the fact that already, right now,
we are the most open healthcare system in

01:49:31.769 --> 01:49:36.139
relation to the quality of hospital care,
for example, of any in the world. That is

01:49:36.139 --> 01:49:41.139
because we believe that we have a right to
hold our health services accountable, precisely

01:49:41.139 --> 01:49:45.349
to hopefully reduce the number of people who
need to make that terrible decision to blow

01:49:45.349 --> 01:49:45.979
the whistle.

01:49:45.979 --> 01:49:50.229
Malcolm Grant: Right. Thank you very much
Tim. I am going to take another three questions

01:49:50.229 --> 01:49:53.769
if I may. The lady there had her hand up,
in the black top there. Thank you.

01:49:53.769 --> 01:49:58.829
Maha Yassaie: I am Maha Yassaie. I used to
work for the NHS, and in 2012 I was sacked

01:49:58.829 --> 01:50:05.829
for whistle blowing. Excuse me if my voice
is shaking because I find it tough to talk

01:50:14.539 --> 01:50:18.610
about it, but I have a question for you.

01:50:18.610 --> 01:50:25.610
I was a proper whistle blower because last
month I had a very good package, with an apology

01:50:26.690 --> 01:50:31.880
from Department of Health, and believe me
I did not want the money. It was not about

01:50:31.880 --> 01:50:38.880
money. The apology was the hardest, it came
from Department of Health, but that is the

01:50:40.380 --> 01:50:45.639
one I wanted. I never signed any gagging,
or would never sign any gagging, because I

01:50:45.639 --> 01:50:51.739
want to be here and I want to be asking you
a question. The people who did what they did

01:50:51.739 --> 01:50:58.739
to me are still in power; they are promoted.
I am asking all of the board members, how

01:51:05.150 --> 01:51:12.150
could you allow that to happen? All I did,
I put a patient ahead of my own career, my

01:51:17.110 --> 01:51:17.489
health.

01:51:17.489 --> 01:51:22.249
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Could I ask you a question?
Was your employer a hospital trust?

01:51:22.249 --> 01:51:27.439
Maha Yassaie: No. I am one of the few; I am
a pharmacist, who worked for a PCT. Probably

01:51:27.439 --> 01:51:31.670
you do not see PCTs as having[?] a whistle
blower, but believe there are those which

01:51:31.670 --> 01:51:36.659
cover up everything. I worked there proudly
for 15 years, but as I said, and at the end

01:51:36.659 --> 01:51:37.039
actually -

01:51:37.039 --> 01:51:38.159
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

01:51:38.159 --> 01:51:41.150
Maha Yassaie: Can I just say another question?

01:51:41.150 --> 01:51:43.130
Malcolm Grant: Is there another question?

01:51:43.130 --> 01:51:46.989
Maha Yassaie: Yes, there is another question,
and unfortunately just a criticism of NHS

01:51:46.989 --> 01:51:47.260
England.

01:51:47.260 --> 01:51:49.469
Professor Sir Malcolm Grant: Is it a question
or criticism?

01:51:49.469 --> 01:51:55.010
Maha Yassaie: It is a question. Why people
get a job in NHS England without that job

01:51:55.010 --> 01:52:02.010
being advertised, without coming for an interview,
and why somebody like me can never get anywhere

01:52:03.659 --> 01:52:08.869
with that kind of job because they are already
for 'one of the boys' and I am not one of

01:52:08.869 --> 01:52:09.119
them?

01:52:09.019 --> 01:52:13.139
Malcolm Grant: Okay, right. We will come back
to that one in a moment.

01:52:13.139 --> 01:52:19.610
Now, question number two. I am conscious there
are bits of the room that I have not really

01:52:19.610 --> 01:52:23.459
been able to explore. There is a gentleman
back there, you sir with your paper in the

01:52:23.459 --> 01:52:26.999
air with the green shirt. Sorry to have to
identify you by your clothing. You, sir?

01:52:26.999 --> 01:52:31.400
Peter McLoughlin: My name is Peter McLoughlin,
lately of Banbury but originally from Lancashire;

01:52:31.400 --> 01:52:38.400
you might be able to tell. My question is
quite a simple one and it is to Mr Dodge.

01:52:38.789 --> 01:52:45.789
Can he sum up his job in a few words, what
it is today and what it might be in five years'

01:52:47.749 --> 01:52:48.429
time?

01:52:48.429 --> 01:52:53.979
Malcolm Grant: Yes. Okay, right. I think I
will land that one on him in a moment if I

01:52:53.979 --> 01:52:55.039
may?

01:52:55.039 --> 01:53:01.719
Let me take another one. Going back, lady
there with the white top I think. You, yes

01:53:01.719 --> 01:53:03.989
please? Sorry, back of three rows. There we
are.

01:53:03.989 --> 01:53:09.589
Caroline: Hi, thank you very much. My name
is Caroline. I worked in mental health for

01:53:03.989 --> 01:53:04.239
quite a long time; I am now in evidence-based
healthcare. I am really ecstatic to hear that

01:53:04.019 --> 01:53:07.300
there is going to be some standards introduced
about access to mental healthcare. Mental

01:53:07.300 --> 01:53:14.300
health is quite unique in the sense that the
criteria for needing treatment and recovering

01:53:27.409 --> 01:53:34.409
from an illness is quite poorly defined. An
example of this would be anorexia, where to

01:53:36.400 --> 01:53:38.829
get access to treatment you need to have quite
severe weight loss. It is usually the point

01:53:38.829 --> 01:53:45.519
where the illness has taken over to the point
where it is very difficult to treat, and these

01:53:45.519 --> 01:53:48.269
patients are often discharged at a point,
based on their BMI, which is often when they

01:53:48.269 --> 01:53:50.202
are at their most vulnerable.

01:53:48.269 --> 01:53:51.709
Malcolm Grant: Caroline, you are leading up
to a question aren't you?

01:53:51.709 --> 01:53:58.709
Caroline: Sorry, I am yeah. This is when you
get this revolving door and I think that is

01:54:01.380 --> 01:54:02.269
probably quite common in mental health. So
my question is, is that something that was

01:54:02.269 --> 01:54:03.699
raised today in the discussions, and will
it be considered as part of the introduction

01:54:03.699 --> 01:54:04.829
to these standards about looking at those
criteria?

01:54:04.829 --> 01:54:07.780
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much for
that. I will take these three questions. I

01:54:07.780 --> 01:54:14.079
think Ian Dodge deserves that first one about
what his job is and what it will be.

01:54:14.079 --> 01:54:21.079
Ian Dodge: Thank you. So I think my job is
to try and help improve healthcare services

01:54:28.219 --> 01:54:33.800
and to help make sure that we end up with
a sustainable NHS, a better NHS for the future,

01:54:33.800 --> 01:54:39.579
and I think that is true also of my fellow
board members. How do I do that? It is by

01:54:39.579 --> 01:54:46.239
helping Simon and the board of NHS England
develop strategy for the NHS. Also, I look

01:54:46.239 --> 01:54:51.840
after - and I am very happy to discuss this
further if you want more detail - the analytical

01:54:51.840 --> 01:54:57.439
teams within NHS England and some of the policy
functions in NHS England. They include things

01:54:57.439 --> 01:55:04.439
like work on tackling inequalities, improving
equality, helping with sustainable development

01:55:06.320 --> 01:55:11.979
in the NHS; there is a number of different
specific things, and as I say, I am very happy

01:55:11.979 --> 01:55:14.969
to discuss that further if that is helpful
to you.

01:55:14.969 --> 01:55:18.760
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. I am going
to then pass on to Simon the question, madam,

01:55:18.760 --> 01:55:25.729
about employment. I think I will look to Bruce,
perhaps, to talk about mental health issues.

01:55:25.729 --> 01:55:26.349
Simon?

01:55:26.349 --> 01:55:32.689
Simon Stevens: Yes. Sorry, I was just trying
to look up the website so I can give you the

01:55:32.689 --> 01:55:36.650
URL where we advertise all our jobs online.
It is NHS Jobs, so it is www.jobs.nhs.uk.

01:55:36.650 --> 01:55:43.650
That is where we post, to Maha's question,
the jobs that we have in NHS England, with

01:55:45.929 --> 01:55:51.170
one principle exception, which is that when
we were set up, a number of the staff that

01:55:51.170 --> 01:55:55.719
we took on were working in other parts of
the NHS, and the agreement that we had with

01:55:55.719 --> 01:56:00.360
the unions, quite rightly, is that if people
were basically doing the same job, rather

01:56:00.360 --> 01:56:05.550
than them being laid off and then having to
start again, they would transfer over. So

01:56:05.550 --> 01:56:10.789
some of that went on, but for the most part
from now on, we are advertising jobs on that

01:56:10.789 --> 01:56:11.249
website.

01:56:11.249 --> 01:56:12.659
Malcolm Grant: Bruce?

01:56:12.659 --> 01:56:19.079
Bruce Keogh: Thank you. Good question about
what we propose to do in terms of access and

01:56:19.079 --> 01:56:23.650
standards in mental health. So there are three
areas that we are looking to focus on at the

01:56:23.650 --> 01:56:29.209
moment, which have emerged through some detailed
discussions and advice.

01:56:29.209 --> 01:56:36.150
The first is to get much quicker treatment
for people who have psychosis. At the moment,

01:56:36.150 --> 01:56:41.349
they are having to wait far too long and we
are keen to really accelerate that. The second

01:56:41.349 --> 01:56:48.349
is to make it much easier for people to access
talking therapies, which have been shown to

01:56:48.969 --> 01:56:54.449
be of enormous benefit to patients; they are
very good value for money and they help get

01:56:54.449 --> 01:57:00.979
people back to work quickly. The third area
that we are focusing on is to try and increase

01:57:00.979 --> 01:57:06.820
the amount of liaison psychiatry in hospitals.
That goes back to the remarks I was making

01:57:06.820 --> 01:57:13.820
earlier about people often present to Accident
&amp; Emergency departments where their prime

01:57:14.209 --> 01:57:20.119
problem is with mental health and, to be honest,
a lot of the staff there are not adequately

01:57:20.119 --> 01:57:23.380
trained for dealing with that kind of thing,
so we want to tackle that.

01:57:23.380 --> 01:57:28.900
You asked very specifically about eating disorders.
The area that we are focusing on eating disorders

01:57:28.900 --> 01:57:35.130
is in Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services,
where that is becoming a growing problem for

01:57:35.130 --> 01:57:40.030
all sorts of reasons that you can imagine
related to social media and peer pressure,

01:57:40.030 --> 01:57:44.030
and what have you. So we are doing some work
on that at the moment. It is not being ignored.

01:57:44.030 --> 01:57:48.360
Malcolm Grant: Ladies and gentlemen, I have
got an apology to make. I have just been handed

01:57:48.360 --> 01:57:53.989
a note that the BSL interpreters have to go
regardless. Can I, on behalf of everybody,

01:57:53.989 --> 01:58:00.989
thank them so much for everything that they
have done. Thank you both; it has been outstanding.

01:58:07.289 --> 01:58:07.889
I am really immensely grateful.

01:58:07.889 --> 01:58:12.030
We have run over time. I am going to take
three more questions and then it is drop everything,

01:58:12.030 --> 01:58:13.959
but three more questions.

01:58:13.959 --> 01:58:20.959
Speaker: Hello. Thank you for this. Can I
begin by saying that I am from Stafford. When

01:58:24.070 --> 01:58:29.780
I hear Simon Stevens talk about NHS reconfiguration
in relationship to Stafford Hospital, I find

01:58:29.780 --> 01:58:33.979
it profoundly disappointing because we were
told that we were put into the unsustainable

01:58:33.979 --> 01:58:39.260
provider's regime after ten years of extremely
hard work, in which Stafford Hospital became

01:58:39.260 --> 01:58:44.269
one of the best performing in the West Midlands.
The reconfiguration in Stafford had nothing

01:58:44.269 --> 01:58:49.530
to do with the history of our town, and to
relate those two is essentially to be blaming

01:58:49.530 --> 01:58:53.429
the people who are the victims of a poor healthcare
ten years ago.

01:58:53.429 --> 01:58:53.769
Malcolm Grant: Sorry, the question?

01:58:53.769 --> 01:58:55.739
Speaker: I think that was a very important
point to make.

01:58:55.739 --> 01:58:57.030
Malcolm Grant: Yeah, but the question?

01:58:57.030 --> 01:59:01.269
Speaker: Okay. So after that, another person
on the board, Ciaran Devane has been, as part

01:59:01.269 --> 01:59:06.349
of Macmillan, responsible for introducing
the largest privatisation in NHS history;

01:59:06.349 --> 01:59:10.909
that has also happened in Staffordshire. There
has been no public consultation, and our CCG

01:59:10.909 --> 01:59:15.809
have told us that they have no statutory duty
to consult or to speak to the patients who

01:59:15.809 --> 01:59:20.320
are affected. I want to ask if that is what
the board intended and if that is acceptable

01:59:20.320 --> 01:59:21.499
to them?

01:59:21.499 --> 01:59:28.499
Malcolm Grant: Right, okay. Sorry, keeping
an eye on time, I think it might be an idea

01:59:31.599 --> 01:59:36.959
if I ask for answers to those two questions.
But can I then assure you that the board will

01:59:36.959 --> 01:59:42.070
be here after I have closed the meeting, and
are happy to talk to everybody individually.

01:59:42.070 --> 01:59:47.199
So let me please ask, first of all, in relation
to reconfiguration, Simon is there anything

01:59:47.199 --> 01:59:48.530
that you would like to add?

01:59:48.530 --> 01:59:51.499
Simon Stevens: Yes. Well, I do not think I
actually mentioned the reconfiguration of

01:59:51.499 --> 01:59:56.539
Staffordshire hospital at all. You may instead
remember that actually I talked about talking

01:59:56.539 --> 02:00:00.889
to the relatives of people who had been affected
by poor quality care that was being delivered

02:00:00.889 --> 02:00:05.010
at Mid Staffordshire hospital, and which has
been the subject of a public inquiry which

02:00:05.010 --> 02:00:09.360
has laid all the facts bare. So that is actually
what I said, I did not talk about the reconfiguration.

02:00:09.360 --> 02:00:14.489
In terms of the reconfiguration though, I
think the broader point is that, actually,

02:00:14.489 --> 02:00:20.679
what is going on is that, in some cases, we
need to make health services more local. That

02:00:20.679 --> 02:00:26.809
means not having centralisation of services.
I personally think that we should see a bigger

02:00:26.809 --> 02:00:31.010
role in some places for local hospitals and
community hospitals, and we have got to challenge

02:00:31.010 --> 02:00:35.489
some of the reasons why that is sometimes
said to be under threat.

02:00:35.489 --> 02:00:40.429
However, in other cases we have actually got
to be honest about the fact that unless we

02:00:40.429 --> 02:00:45.639
make changes, patients are going to suffer.
I will give you just one very concrete example

02:00:45.639 --> 02:00:52.639
of that. Anywhere in England now if you are
riding a motorbike and you are hit by a bus

02:00:52.849 --> 02:00:59.849
and come off, you will be taken by the ambulance
not necessarily to the nearest A&amp;E department,

02:01:00.869 --> 02:01:06.840
you will be taken to an A&amp;E department where
they have got a major trauma centre. The consequence

02:01:06.840 --> 02:01:13.840
of that is 600 of our fellow citizens are
now alive two years after this change came

02:01:14.469 --> 02:01:16.739
in who would otherwise have been dead.

02:01:16.739 --> 02:01:21.639
So we have just got to, sometimes, be honest
about the fact that unless we make some of

02:01:21.639 --> 02:01:26.999
these changes, care will be worse. But we
have got to also make sure that, when we are

02:01:26.999 --> 02:01:32.139
making these changes, we are very straight
up about why it is occurring.

02:01:32.139 --> 02:01:36.380
Speaker: What about the people who are going
to die because of the A&amp;E closures?

02:01:36.380 --> 02:01:39.889
Simon Stevens: Well, okay. So let's just talk
about that. I am going to ask Bruce because

02:01:39.889 --> 02:01:45.699
Bruce has actually been leading a review on
emergency care, and what is being proposed

02:01:45.699 --> 02:01:51.530
is actually not a series of mass A&amp;E closures
across the country but instead making sure

02:01:51.530 --> 02:01:56.199
that the major specialist centres have got
the extra services needed in order to deal

02:01:56.199 --> 02:01:57.619
with this high complexity care.

02:01:57.619 --> 02:01:58.900
Speaker: What, like in Lewisham?

02:01:58.900 --> 02:02:01.769
Simon Stevens: Well, let's just listen to
Bruce because Bruce actually knows what he

02:02:01.769 --> 02:02:06.780
is talking about on this one, even if you
think I do not.

02:02:06.780 --> 02:02:13.780
Bruce Keogh: Firstly, Simon's alluded to major
trauma. There are two other areas where it

02:02:15.959 --> 02:02:20.150
is important that we get people to places
where they can be treated properly. One is

02:02:20.150 --> 02:02:24.840
with heart attacks, because the science of
treatment of heart attacks has advanced dramatically,

02:02:24.840 --> 02:02:30.769
and the second is Stroke. That constitutes,
if you add all of those together, about 5%

02:02:30.769 --> 02:02:37.769
of the emergencies that are dealt with in
our NHS. So we have shown a very dramatic

02:02:39.079 --> 02:02:43.050
reduction in mortality for heart attacks,
which has been incremental over the course

02:02:43.050 --> 02:02:50.010
of the last few years, from 25%, as you mentioned,
down to around about 5% now. I think we have

02:02:50.010 --> 02:02:54.110
got to a really good place in terms of international
comparisons.

02:02:54.110 --> 02:02:58.559
Similarly in London, we reduced the number
of places that would take people with acute

02:02:58.559 --> 02:03:04.369
strokes from 32 down to eight. There were
a lot of people who were concerned, if the

02:03:04.369 --> 02:03:10.300
ambulances went past somewhere, that people
would die en route. In the same way they had

02:03:10.300 --> 02:03:17.130
similar concerns about trauma. The reality
is that now we have a shorter length of stay

02:03:17.130 --> 02:03:21.849
for people who are admitted with strokes,
a lower mortality and more people returning

02:03:21.849 --> 02:03:28.849
back to independent living than before those
particular, clinically driven different ways

02:03:31.650 --> 02:03:37.229
of delivering the service. Furthermore, it
is estimated that it will save tens of millions

02:03:37.229 --> 02:03:38.590
of pounds.

02:03:38.590 --> 02:03:44.489
So what we have got to really focus on is
how we deliver the best possible clinical

02:03:44.489 --> 02:03:50.829
outcomes to people who are sick, worried and
anxious, and simply want to get better. Sometimes

02:03:50.829 --> 02:03:57.539
that does mean changing the way we deliver
our services. In the review, which I have

02:03:57.539 --> 02:04:01.469
been overseeing and which has been led by
my colleague Keith Willett, who is Professor

02:04:01.469 --> 02:04:08.469
of Trauma in Oxford, we are quite clear that
people want services close to home, and for

02:04:10.389 --> 02:04:17.389
the vast majority of things that people attend
A&amp;E, walk-in centres and other things for,

02:04:17.449 --> 02:04:22.170
they can be dealt with close to home. We want
to encourage that, and we want to promote

02:04:22.170 --> 02:04:27.479
those services. But we also have to be absolutely
honest about what the offer is, because there

02:04:27.479 --> 02:04:33.979
is a tendency to think that all A&amp;Es are equal,
whereas they are not. We know that about 1

02:04:33.979 --> 02:04:39.229
in 5 or 1 in 7 of them does not have some
very basic facilities that many people would

02:04:39.229 --> 02:04:43.269
consider are essential for an A&amp;E.

02:04:43.269 --> 02:04:50.269
We also know that there is confusion over
what a walk-in centre is, a GP-led centre;

02:04:50.880 --> 02:04:55.659
there are something like 22 different names.
What we want to do is we want to standardise

02:04:55.659 --> 02:05:00.439
the names, we want to be absolutely transparent
about what the offer is in each A&amp;E, and we

02:05:00.439 --> 02:05:05.459
also want to ensure that where specialist
services offer a particularly good and better

02:05:05.459 --> 02:05:10.219
outcome for patients, the patients get to
the specialist centres as quickly as possible.

02:05:10.219 --> 02:05:13.300
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much Bruce.
Before I pass over to Ciaran to answer the

02:05:13.300 --> 02:05:17.289
last question, I just want to express thanks
on behalf of all of us.

02:05:17.289 --> 02:05:17.809
Speaker: [Inaudible]

02:05:17.809 --> 02:05:22.209
Malcolm Grant: I am passing over to Ciaran
in a moment, madam. I just want, before I

02:05:22.209 --> 02:05:28.090
do that, to pass on our grateful thanks to
the speech to text reporters and the graphic

02:05:28.090 --> 02:05:32.760
reporters. Again, I have run over time, it
is my fault, it is not theirs, but please

02:05:32.760 --> 02:05:39.760
can we thank them very much for the extraordinary
work today.

02:05:41.150 --> 02:05:47.249
Ciaran, your responsibilities are wearing
a different hat, but I think if you could

02:05:47.249 --> 02:05:51.280
give a brief account then that will conclude
the meeting. Thank you.

02:05:51.280 --> 02:05:56.300
Ciaran Devane: Yeah. Four of the five CCGs
in Staffordshire - population about three

02:05:56.300 --> 02:06:02.619
quarters of a million - talked to cancer patients
in Staffordshire and they were told two things.

02:06:02.619 --> 02:06:07.510
One was coordination is rubbish. You go into
a hospital, you get bounced to a GP, you get

02:06:07.510 --> 02:06:11.909
sent to a hospice, and you are back into hospital,
so they said they wanted to do something about

02:06:11.909 --> 02:06:15.939
it. The other thing they said was, out of
three quarters of a million, if they get this

02:06:15.939 --> 02:06:20.189
right there will be 1,400 people in Staffordshire
alive after a year who would otherwise be

02:06:20.189 --> 02:06:20.800
dead.

02:06:20.800 --> 02:06:26.320
So they came to us, with my day job hat on,
and they said, 'Actually, we want to make

02:06:26.320 --> 02:06:32.189
sure the patient voice is in the room. We
know we will get resistance from the local

02:06:32.189 --> 02:06:37.269
NHS hospitals or whoever about this, and we
want to make sure the patient voice is in

02:06:37.269 --> 02:06:44.269
the room.' So what they want to do is to stop
managing 620 contracts and focus on the quality

02:06:44.360 --> 02:06:48.979
of care for their people. What they said was
they want to go to the providers with the

02:06:48.979 --> 02:06:54.219
NHS hospitals in Staffordshire and say, 'We
will give you a contract, not ten for one

02:06:54.219 --> 02:06:59.820
year but we will give one contract for ten
years, but you have to promise to play nicely

02:06:59.820 --> 02:07:05.919
with each other and get that coordination
right.' Now, there is no privatisation involved.

02:07:05.919 --> 02:07:12.900
So the issue is how do we get that coordination?
How do we save 1,400 lives? How will we get

02:07:12.900 --> 02:07:14.689
the hospital and the hospice -

02:07:14.689 --> 02:07:14.939
Speaker: [Inaudible].

02:07:14.889 --> 02:07:18.249
Ciaran Devane: I completely agree. I will
go away and I will check that because the

02:07:18.249 --> 02:07:21.510
CCGs are telling me that they are talking
to the public a lot, so I would love to talk

02:07:21.510 --> 02:07:23.590
to you afterwards and see what is really going
on.

02:07:23.590 --> 02:07:24.669
Speaker: [Inaudible].

02:07:24.669 --> 02:07:29.590
Ciaran Devane: I will happily talk to you
afterwards and you can tell me. It is really

02:07:29.590 --> 02:07:33.800
interesting because we know that there are
people on the radio in Staffordshire, we know

02:07:33.800 --> 02:07:37.959
that there are people out holding public meetings
in Staffordshire, so I would love to understand

02:07:37.959 --> 02:07:43.860
what the issue really is. But 1,400 people
will be alive after a year who would otherwise

02:07:43.860 --> 02:07:45.789
be dead and that is worth fighting for.

02:07:45.789 --> 02:07:49.179
Malcolm Grant: Right, thank you very much.
Ladies and gentlemen, this has been an exceptional

02:07:49.179 --> 02:07:55.679
day for us, an exceptional day for the NHS
citizens who have participated in our workshops,

02:07:55.679 --> 02:08:02.090
and can I say how grateful I am to everybody
who has come tonight. Can I also say I will

02:08:02.090 --> 02:08:03.689
wear this with pride.

02:08:03.689 --> 02:08:04.309
Speaker: Liar!

02:08:04.309 --> 02:08:08.099
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. The meeting
is closed. Thank you very much.

